{"data":[{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05834","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05834","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05834","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tony","familyName":"Lindeberg","name":"Lindeberg, Tony","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Time-causal and time-recursive wavelets"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Signal Processing (eess.SP)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Numerical Analysis (math.NA)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Systems and Control (eess.SY)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Image and Video Processing (eess.IV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T11:59:36Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-10T00:41:19Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-16T07:50:05Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-17T00:34:13Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-22T10:07:10Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10-23T00:37:32Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10-24T04:45:13Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2025-10-27T00:24:26Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2025-11-18T14:15:07Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v5"},{"date":"2025-11-19T01:51:25Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v5"},{"date":"2025-11-25T08:29:38Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v6"},{"date":"2025-11-26T01:38:03Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v6"},{"date":"2025-12-01T05:39:47Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v7"},{"date":"2025-12-02T02:10:29Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v7"},{"date":"2025-12-09T07:44:49Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v8"},{"date":"2025-12-10T01:30:08Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v8"},{"date":"2026-01-13T07:16:55Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v9"},{"date":"2026-01-14T01:25:22Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v9"},{"date":"2026-04-18T06:02:42Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v10"},{"date":"2026-10-21T00:45:22Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v10"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.3389/frsip.2026.1786202","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"10","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"This paper presents a framework for time-causal wavelet analysis. It targets real-time processing of temporal signals, where data from the future are not available.\n The study builds upon temporal scale-space theory, originating from a complete classification of temporal smoothing kernels that guarantee non-creation of new structures from finer to coarser temporal scale levels. We construct temporal wavelets from the temporal derivatives of a special time-causal smoothing kernel, referred to as the time-causal limit kernel, as arising from the classification of variation-diminishing smoothing transformations with the complementary requirement of temporal scale covariance, to guarantee self-similar handling of structures in the input signal at different temporal scales. This enables decomposition of the signal into different components at different scales, while adhering to temporal causality.\n The paper establishes theoretical foundations for these time-causal wavelet representations, and maps structural relationships to the non-causal Ricker or Mexican hat wavelets. We also describe how efficient discrete approximations of the presented theory can be performed in terms of first-order recursive filters coupled in cascade, which enables numerically well-conditioned real-time processing with low resource usage. We characterize and quantify how the continuous scaling properties transfer to the discrete implementation, demonstrating how the proposed time-causal wavelet representation can reflect the duration of locally dominant temporal structures in the input signal."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"33 pages, 13 figures, 1 table, 2 algorithm boxes"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05834","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":11,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2025-10-08T01:58:05Z","registered":"2025-10-08T01:58:06Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-10-21T03:13:46Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04914","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04914","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04914","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Maximilian","familyName":"Delto","name":"Delto, Maximilian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alexander","familyName":"Penin","name":"Penin, Alexander","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lorenzo","familyName":"Tancredi","name":"Tancredi, Lorenzo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"High-Energy Evolution of Power-Suppressed Amplitudes"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"High Energy Physics - Phenomenology (hep-ph)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T15:30:02Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:40:47Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T12:57:15Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-10T00:53:49Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-10-20T16:42:19Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-10-21T01:30:36Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We present a new class of evolution equations which govern the high-energy behavior of power-suppressed scattering amplitudes. The equations can be viewed as a renormalization group flow with respect to the relevant effective field theory cutoff. A distinct feature of the method is in the use of a multidimensional cutoff to separate the relevant scales in problems characterized by a complex factorization structure. By adjusting the renormalization group variables to the geometry of the effective theory modes, our method naturally extends to a broad spectrum of physical problems including massive, massless, small, and wide angle scattering. We present applications to the benchmark processes of electron-positron forward annihilation and light quark mediated Higgs boson production/decays."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"7 pages, 2 figures. Changed IR regularization scheme for NLL evolution kernel in the case of electron-muon forward annihilation. Solution now retains full dependence on the electron-to-muon mass ratio"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04914","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T16:58:32Z","registered":"2025-10-07T16:58:33Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-10-21T03:13:45Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05632","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05632","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05632","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tianhao","familyName":"Zhu","name":"Zhu, Tianhao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dahu","familyName":"Feng","name":"Feng, Dahu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Erhu","familyName":"Feng","name":"Feng, Erhu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yubin","familyName":"Xia","name":"Xia, Yubin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Systematic Exploration of Multi-core Architectures for Efficient LLM Serving using WaferAI-SIM"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Hardware Architecture (cs.AR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T07:29:16Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:29:49Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-09-28T12:18:15Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-09-29T02:52:03Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1109/tcad.2026.3719164","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"With the widespread adoption of Large Language Models (LLMs), the demand for high-performance LLM inference services continues to grow. Multi-core AI accelerators, such as Groq, Graphcore IPU, and Cerebras WSE, provide promising platforms for LLM serving, but their distributed memory systems require careful coordination between hardware configuration and serving policies. Otherwise, mismatched tensor partitioning, data placement, and memory management can substantially underutilize compute and communication resources.\n To address these challenges, we present WaferAI-SIM, a multi-level simulation framework that combines transaction-level simulation with an analytical performance model. WaferAI-SIM enables simulator-driven co-design of LLM serving strategies and multi-core accelerator architectures, targeting the early design stage in which emerging platforms are not yet broadly available for empirical serving studies. It captures how LLM serving policies interact with compute-core count, memory hierarchy, and interconnect topology, enabling architecture-aware exploration beyond GPU-centric assumptions. We evaluate representative LLMs across a range of chip configurations and serving scenarios. Across the evaluation, WaferAI-SIM reports 1.32$\\times$--6.03$\\times$ latency improvements, where the lower endpoint comes from ring-based placement at TP=16 over the placement baselines, and the upper endpoint comes from K-dimension TP over MN-dimension TP for Qwen3\\_4B at TP=4 with sequence length 256. For LLM serving, our findings provide guidance for co-designing hardware architectures and serving strategies for multi-core AI accelerators across diverse LLM workloads."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05632","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2025-10-08T01:53:23Z","registered":"2025-10-08T01:53:24Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-09-29T06:56:53Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05111","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05111","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05111","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ian","familyName":"McDougall","name":"McDougall, Ian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Noah","familyName":"Scott","name":"Scott, Noah","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Karthikeyan","familyName":"Sankaralingam","name":"Sankaralingam, Karthikeyan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Agora: A System for Consumption-Based GPU Pricing"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Distributed, Parallel, and Cluster Computing (cs.DC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-26T15:24:06Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:00:16Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-09-22T19:05:28Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-09-25T00:01:46Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We present Agora, a lightweight system designed for cloud-based GPUs which meters a three-dimensional resource vector from commodity performance counters and prices it with a function that charges customers based on resources used rather than time spent renting. Because the calibration is revenue-neutral, any gain must come from demand the flat rate had priced out, and we report under what conditions this consumption-based pricing scheme increases revenue: we find that the switch is revenue-negative at the median on a fixed market, but provides benefits in 86 percent of cases where a provider serves 15 to 30 percent of its addressable market against 10 percent where it serves over 60 percent. Revenue expands dramatically in proportion to the number of customers priced out by the flat rate: up to 141 percent in an optimistic case."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"17 pages, 4 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05111","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T01:41:21Z","registered":"2025-10-08T01:41:21Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-09-25T02:51:43Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05206","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05206","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05206","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Florian","familyName":"List","name":"List, Florian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Oliver","familyName":"Hahn","name":"Hahn, Oliver","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Thomas","familyName":"Flöss","name":"Flöss, Thomas","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lukas","familyName":"Winkler","name":"Winkler, Lukas","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"DISCO-DJ II: a differentiable particle-mesh code for cosmology"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Cosmology and Nongalactic Astrophysics (astro-ph.CO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Instrumentation and Methods for Astrophysics (astro-ph.IM)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T18:00:05Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-23T00:43:16Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1088/1475-7516/2026/06/033","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The mildly non-linear regime of cosmic structure formation holds much of the information that upcoming large-scale structure surveys aim to exploit, making fast and accurate predictions on these scales essential. We present the $N$-body module of DISCO-DJ (DIfferentiable Simulations for COsmology - Done with Jax), designed to deliver high-fidelity, GPU-accelerated, and differentiable particle-mesh simulations tailored for cosmological inference. Theory-informed time integrators such as the recently introduced BullFrog method allow for accurate predictions already with few time steps (e.g. $6$ steps for per-cent-level accuracy in terms of the present-day power spectrum at $k \\approx 0.2 \\, h / \\mathrm{Mpc}$ using $N = 512^3$ particles, which takes just a few seconds). To control discreteness effects and achieve high accuracy, the code incorporates a suite of advanced techniques, for example a custom non-uniform FFT implementation for force evaluation. Both forward- and reverse-mode differentiation are supported, with memory requirements independent of the number of time steps; in the reverse case, this is achieved through an adjoint formulation. We extensively study the effect of various numerical parameters on the accuracy. As an application of DISCO-DJ, we perform field-level inference by recovering $σ_8$ and the initial conditions from a noisy Gadget matter density field. Coupled with our recently introduced Einstein--Boltzmann solver, the DISCO-DJ ecosystem provides a self-consistent, fully differentiable pipeline for modelling the large-scale structure of the universe. The code is available at https://github.com/cosmo-sims/DISCO-DJ."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"37 + 19 pages, 13 + 7 figures, to be submitted to JCAP. Comments welcome"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05206","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2025-10-08T01:43:32Z","registered":"2025-10-08T01:43:33Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-23T05:35:00Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05255","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05255","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05255","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Farhad","familyName":"Rezazadeh","name":"Rezazadeh, Farhad","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hatim","familyName":"Chergui","name":"Chergui, Hatim","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Merouane","familyName":"Debbah","name":"Debbah, Merouane","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Houbing","familyName":"Song","name":"Song, Houbing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lingjia","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Lingjia","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Rivaling Transformers: Multi-Scale Structured State-Space Mixtures for Agentic 6G O-RAN"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Networking and Internet Architecture (cs.NI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T18:17:29Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:04:15Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-06-08T07:39:19Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-06-09T01:27:50Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"In sixth-generation (6G) Open Radio Access Networks (O-RAN), proactive control is preferable. A key open challenge is delivering control-grade predictions within Near-Real-Time (Near-RT) latency and computational constraints under multi-timescale dynamics. We therefore cast RAN Intelligent Controller (RIC) analytics as an agentic perceive-predict xApp that turns noisy, multivariate RAN telemetry into short-horizon per-User Equipment (UE) key performance indicator (KPI) forecasts to drive anticipatory control. In this regard, Transformers are powerful for sequence learning and time-series forecasting, but they are memory-intensive, which limits Near-RT RIC use. Therefore, we need models that maintain accuracy while reducing latency and data movement. To this end, we propose a lightweight Multi-Scale Structured State-Space Mixtures (MS3M) forecaster that mixes HiPPO-LegS kernels to capture multi-timescale radio dynamics. We develop stable discrete state-space models (SSMs) via bilinear (Tustin) discretization and apply their causal impulse responses as per-feature depthwise convolutions. Squeeze-and-Excitation gating dynamically reweights KPI channels as conditions change, and a compact gated channel-mixing layer models cross-feature nonlinearities without Transformer-level cost. The model is KPI-agnostic -- Reference Signal Received Power (RSRP) serves as a canonical use case -- and is trained on sliding windows to predict the immediate next step. Empirical evaluations conducted using our bespoke O-RAN testbed KPI time-series dataset (59,441 windows across 13 KPIs). Crucially for O-RAN constraints, MS3M achieves a 0.057 s per-inference latency with 0.70M parameters, yielding 3-10x lower latency than the Transformer baselines evaluated on the same hardware, while maintaining competitive accuracy."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"12 pages, 2 Figures, 5 Tables"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05255","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T01:44:41Z","registered":"2025-10-08T01:44:41Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-06-09T04:13:03Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05991","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05991","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05991","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Matias D.","familyName":"Cattaneo","name":"Cattaneo, Matias D.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Michael","familyName":"Jansson","name":"Jansson, Michael","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kenichi","familyName":"Nagasawa","name":"Nagasawa, Kenichi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Robust Inference for Convex Pairwise Difference Estimators"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Econometrics (econ.EM)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Statistics Theory (math.ST)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Methodology (stat.ME)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Economics and business","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Economics and business","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T14:51:05Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:52:51Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-05-28T14:10:03Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-05-29T01:08:45Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"This paper develops distribution theory and bootstrap-based inference methods for a broad class of convex pairwise difference estimators. These estimators minimize a kernel-weighted convex-in-parameter function over observation pairs with similar covariates, where the similarity is governed by a localization (bandwidth) parameter. While classical results establish asymptotic normality under restrictive bandwidth conditions, we show that valid Gaussian and bootstrap-based inference remains possible under substantially weaker assumptions. First, we extend the theory of small bandwidth asymptotics to convex pairwise difference estimation settings, deriving robust Gaussian approximations even when a smaller than standard bandwidth is used. Second, we employ a debiasing procedure based on generalized jackknifing to enable inference with larger bandwidths, while preserving convexity of the objective function. Third, we construct a novel bootstrap method that adjusts for bandwidth-induced variance distortions, yielding valid inference across a wide range of bandwidth choices. Our proposed inference method enjoys demonstrably greater robustness, while retaining the practical appeal of convex pairwise difference estimators."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05991","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T02:01:49Z","registered":"2025-10-08T02:01:50Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-05-29T02:57:05Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05497","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05497","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05497","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhongkai","familyName":"Yu","name":"Yu, Zhongkai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yue","familyName":"Guan","name":"Guan, Yue","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zihao","familyName":"Yu","name":"Yu, Zihao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chenyang","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Chenyang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhengding","familyName":"Hu","name":"Hu, Zhengding","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shuyi","familyName":"Pei","name":"Pei, Shuyi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yangwook","familyName":"Kang","name":"Kang, Yangwook","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yufei","familyName":"Ding","name":"Ding, Yufei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Po-An","familyName":"Tsai","name":"Tsai, Po-An","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Patterns behind Chaos: Forecasting Data Movement for Efficient Large-Scale MoE LLM Inference"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Distributed, Parallel, and Cluster Computing (cs.DC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Hardware Architecture (cs.AR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T01:31:39Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:17:33Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-12-04T09:23:08Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-12-05T01:31:20Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-12-05T07:59:52Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-12-08T01:23:06Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-04-02T18:32:07Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-04-06T00:04:19Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-05-12T10:04:18Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v5"},{"date":"2026-05-13T00:54:15Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v5"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"5","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large-scale Mixture of Experts (MoE) Large Language Models (LLMs) have recently become the frontier open-weight models, achieving remarkable model capability similar to proprietary ones. But their random expert selection mechanism introduces significant data movement overhead that becomes the dominant bottleneck in multi-unit LLM serving systems.\n To understand the patterns underlying this data movement, we conduct comprehensive data-movement-centric profiling across four state-of-the-art large-scale MoE models released in 2025 (200B-1000B) using over 24,000 requests spanning diverse workloads. We perform systematic analysis from both temporal and spatial perspectives and distill six key insights to guide the design of diverse serving systems. We verify these insights on both future wafer-scale GPU architectures and existing GPU systems. On wafer-scale GPUs, lightweight architectural modifications guided by our insights yield a 6.6$\\times$ average speedup across four 200B--1000B models. On existing GPU systems, our insights drive the design of a prefill-aware expert placement algorithm that achieves up to 1.25$\\times$ speedup on MoE computation. Our work presents the first comprehensive data-centric analysis of large-scale MoE models together with a concrete design study applying the learned lessons. Our profiling traces are publicly available at \\href{https://huggingface.co/datasets/core12345/MoE_expert_selection_trace}{\\textcolor{blue}{https://huggingface.co/datasets/core12345/MoE\\_expert\\_selection\\_trace}}."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05497","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":4,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T01:50:14Z","registered":"2025-10-08T01:50:15Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-05-13T02:57:35Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05109","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05109","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05109","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yilong","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Yilong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shuai","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Shuai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yijing","familyName":"Zeng","name":"Zeng, Yijing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hao","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Hao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xinmiao","familyName":"Xiong","name":"Xiong, Xinmiao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jingyu","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Jingyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Pan","familyName":"Hu","name":"Hu, Pan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Suman","familyName":"Banerjee","name":"Banerjee, Suman","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Tiny but Mighty: A Software-Hardware Co-Design Approach for Efficient Multimodal Inference on Battery-Powered Small Devices"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Distributed, Parallel, and Cluster Computing (cs.DC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Signal Processing (eess.SP)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-25T22:28:44Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:00:12Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-27T14:17:43Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-28T01:46:06Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-12-01T19:27:54Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-12-03T01:03:38Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-03-02T05:38:29Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-03-03T02:41:07Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-03-06T20:25:12Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v5"},{"date":"2026-03-10T00:09:54Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v5"},{"date":"2026-05-02T08:53:15Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v6"},{"date":"2026-05-05T00:26:44Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v6"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"6","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large Multimodal Models (LMMs) are inherently modular, comprising vision and audio encoders, a projector, and a language backbone. Yet existing systems execute them monolithically, underutilizing the heterogeneous accelerators (NPUs, GPUs, DSPs) on modern SoCs and inflating end-to-end latency. We present Nanomind, a hardware-software co-design inference framework that decomposes each LMM into modular \"bricks\"--vision, projector, language, and audio--and maps each brick to its best-suited compute units. A Token-Aware Buffer Manager (TABM) enables zero-copy embedding transfer across accelerators on unified-memory SoCs, bypassing CPU bottlenecks. Combined with customized hardware, a battery-aware scheduler, and fused low-bit GEMM kernels, Nanomind runs entirely on a compact, battery-powered prototype that operates fully offline. Nanomind reduces end-to-end energy by 42.3% against mainstream edge frameworks and devkits; in its on-demand low-power mode, the prototype runs LLaVA-OneVision-Qwen2-0.5B with a camera for nearly 18.8 hours on a single 2,000 mAh battery."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05109","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":5,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T01:41:18Z","registered":"2025-10-08T01:41:19Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-05-05T04:00:54Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05523","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05523","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05523","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Akatsuki","familyName":"Nishioka","name":"Nishioka, Akatsuki","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Revisiting Invex Functions: Explicit Kernel Constructions and Characterizations"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Optimization and Control (math.OC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T02:28:21Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:19:37Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-04-03T07:56:35Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-04-06T00:30:24Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"An invex function generalizes a convex function in the sense that every stationary point is a global minimizer. Recently, invex functions and their subclasses have attracted attention in signal processing and machine learning. However, verifying invexity is often difficult because its definition involves an unknown function called a kernel function. This paper studies kernel functions associated with invex functions, which have received relatively limited attention in the literature. In particular, we develop several methods for constructing explicit kernel functions and establish a characterization of pseudoconvexity in terms of kernel functions. These results provide constructive tools for proving invexity of new functions and for clarifying their structural properties. We also present examples of nonsmooth, non-pseudoconvex invex functions arising in signal processing."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05523","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T01:50:51Z","registered":"2025-10-08T01:50:52Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-04-06T01:49:14Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06122","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06122","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06122","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Markus","familyName":"Krimmel","name":"Krimmel, Markus","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Philip","familyName":"Hartout","name":"Hartout, Philip","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Karsten","familyName":"Borgwardt","name":"Borgwardt, Karsten","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dexiong","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Dexiong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"PolyGraph Discrepancy: a classifier-based metric for graph generation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (stat.ML)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T17:02:44Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:59:45Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-03-17T07:36:37Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-18T00:40:49Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Existing methods for evaluating graph generative models primarily rely on Maximum Mean Discrepancy (MMD) metrics based on graph descriptors. While these metrics can rank generative models, they do not provide an absolute measure of performance. Their values are also highly sensitive to extrinsic parameters, namely kernel and descriptor parametrization, making them incomparable across different graph descriptors. We introduce PolyGraph Discrepancy (PGD), a new evaluation framework that addresses these limitations. It approximates the Jensen-Shannon distance of graph distributions by fitting binary classifiers to distinguish between real and generated graphs, featurized by these descriptors. The data log-likelihood of these classifiers approximates a variational lower bound on the JS distance between the two distributions. Resulting metrics are constrained to the unit interval [0,1] and are comparable across different graph descriptors. We further derive a theoretically grounded summary metric that combines these individual metrics to provide a maximally tight lower bound on the distance for the given descriptors. Thorough experiments demonstrate that PGD provides a more robust and insightful evaluation compared to MMD metrics. The PolyGraph framework for benchmarking graph generative models is made publicly available at https://github.com/BorgwardtLab/polygraph-benchmark."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Camera-ready version published at ICLR 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06122","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T02:04:52Z","registered":"2025-10-08T02:04:53Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-03-18T02:49:29Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05842","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05842","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05842","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Najmeh","familyName":"Mirian","name":"Mirian, Najmeh","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Short-Pulse High-Power THz Generation Using Optical Klystron FELs: Simulation Results"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Accelerator Physics (physics.acc-ph)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T12:05:28Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:43:14Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-03-09T10:12:49Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-10T01:58:09Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The generation of high-power radiation in the terahertz (THz) regime using free-electron lasers (FELs) is challenging due to strong diffraction and pronounced slippage effects. These constraints often limit the achievable pulse duration and peak power in conventional single-pass THz FEL configurations. In this work, we investigate an unseeded optical klystron (OK) FEL concept tailored for the THz regime. Using time-dependent three-dimensional simulations for resonant wavelengths of 10, 30, and 100 μm, we demonstrate that this approach enables the generation of coherent ultrashort THz pulses with durations of a sub picoseconds (FWHM) and peak powers in the multi-hundred- megawatt range at wavelengths 10 and 30 μm. To address the slippage challenge, we propose and numerically demonstrate a novel chicane-embedded optical delay scheme, which restores phase alignment between the radiation and microbunched electrons. Simulations confirm that careful tuning of the dispersive strengths allows staged amplification, preserving beam quality and reaching multi-megawatt output power. These results highlight the potential of THz-tailored optical klystrons to generate compact, short, and high-intensity THz pulses, and lay the groundwork for future experimental studies and facility implementation"},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"11 pages, 9 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05842","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T01:58:17Z","registered":"2025-10-08T01:58:18Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-03-10T04:51:01Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06219","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06219","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06219","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yue","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Yue","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xingyu","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Xingyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuxuan","familyName":"Xue","name":"Xue, Yuxuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Anpei","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Anpei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuliang","familyName":"Xiu","name":"Xiu, Yuliang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Gerard","familyName":"Pons-Moll","name":"Pons-Moll, Gerard","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Human3R: Everyone Everywhere All at Once"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T17:59:52Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T01:04:06Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-03-03T12:42:36Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-03-04T01:48:51Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We present Human3R, a unified, feed-forward framework for online 4D human-scene reconstruction, in the world frame, from casually captured monocular videos. Unlike previous approaches that rely on multi-stage pipelines, iterative contact-aware refinement between humans and scenes, and heavy dependencies, e.g., human detection, depth estimation, and SLAM pre-processing, Human3R jointly recovers global multi-person SMPL-X bodies (\"everyone\"), dense 3D scene (\"everywhere\"), and camera trajectories in a single forward pass (\"all-at-once\"). Our method builds upon the 4D online reconstruction model CUT3R, and uses parameter-efficient visual prompt tuning, to strive to preserve CUT3R's rich spatiotemporal priors, while enabling direct readout of multiple SMPL-X bodies. Human3R is a unified model that eliminates heavy dependencies and iterative refinement. After being trained on the relatively small-scale synthetic dataset BEDLAM for just one day on one GPU, it achieves superior performance with remarkable efficiency: it reconstructs multiple humans in a one-shot manner, along with 3D scenes, in one stage, in real-time (15 FPS) with a low memory footprint (8 GB). Extensive experiments demonstrate that Human3R delivers state-of-the-art or competitive performance across tasks, including global human motion estimation, local human mesh recovery, video depth estimation, and camera pose estimation, with a single unified model. We hope that Human3R will serve as a simple yet strong baseline, which can be easily adapted for downstream applications. Code, models and 4D interactive demos are available at https://fanegg.github.io/Human3R/."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Page: https://fanegg.github.io/Human3R Code: https://github.com/fanegg/Human3R"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06219","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T02:07:10Z","registered":"2025-10-08T02:07:11Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-03-04T03:18:37Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05507","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05507","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05507","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Grigor","familyName":"Tukharyan","name":"Tukharyan, Grigor","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"William Reed","familyName":"Kendrick","name":"Kendrick, William Reed","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Areg","familyName":"Danagoulian","name":"Danagoulian, Areg","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Benoit","familyName":"Forget","name":"Forget, Benoit","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Prediction of Spallation Induced Transmutation Rates For Long Lived Fission Products via Proton Accelerator"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Accelerator Physics (physics.acc-ph)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Nuclear Experiment (nucl-ex)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T01:51:34Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:18:05Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T18:32:39Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-13T00:04:58Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02-19T21:31:15Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-02-23T01:07:45Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1038/s41598-026-38736-9","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Long lived fission products represent a major challenge in nuclear waste management due to persistent radiotoxicity over very long timescales. This study focuses on six of these fission products: Se-79, Zr-93, Tc-99, Sn-126, I-127, Cs-135. This study investigates the feasibility of spallation driven transmutation, in which a high energy proton beam strikes a heavy spallation target to generate neutrons that induce transmutation in the fission products surrounding the target. Lead and depleted uranium are identified as the principal spallation target candidates, reflecting contrasting trade offs in neutron yield, secondary reactions, and heat generation. Simulations assess nuclide specific behavior under reactor scale inventories and practical geometric constraints. Results demonstrate that technetium, iodine, and selenium are strong candidates for transmutation using this pathway, while tin shows partial resistance but benefits from thermal flux. By contrast, zirconium is neutron transparent and inefficient to transmute, and cesium suffers from low net reduction due to competition with lighter isotopes. Cost effectiveness is highly isotope dependent: technetium is most favorable, whereas cesium and zirconium remain expensive. These findings highlight the advantages and limitations of spallation driven systems and motivate strategies with optimized target-blanket designs."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"12 pages, 6 tables, 4 figures. Submitted to Scientific Reports. Original submission replaced to add an author"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05507","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2025-10-08T01:50:28Z","registered":"2025-10-08T01:50:29Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-23T02:45:32Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05594","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05594","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05594","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Takao","familyName":"Tomono","name":"Tomono, Takao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kazuya","familyName":"Tsujimura","name":"Tsujimura, Kazuya","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Quantum Kernel Anomaly Detection Using AR-Derived Features from Non-Contact Acoustic Monitoring for Smart Manufacturing"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Quantum Physics (quant-ph)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T05:42:44Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-02-13T01:13:08Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1109/qce65121.2025.00195.","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The evolution of manufacturing toward smart factories has underscored major challenges in equipment maintenance, particularly the dependence on numerous contact sensors for anomaly detection, leading to increased sensor complexity and computational costs. This study explores the use of quantum kernels to enhance anomaly detection based on noncontact sensors. We hypothesize that the expressive power of quantum feature spaces can effectively discriminate among multiple anomaly types using fewer sensors. Experiments were conducted on two types of manufacturing equipment a conveyor and a chain belt machine where a single directional microphone was placed at varying distances (0 to 3 m) to capture audio data. The signals were processed using autoregressive (AR) modeling to extract coefficient based features, which were then mapped into quantum feature space via quantum kernels for one class SVM classification. The quantum kernel classifiers achieved consistently high accuracy and F1 scores (more than 0.92) across all distances, while classical counterparts exhibited significant degradation beyond 0 m. Visualization of the feature space revealed clear separability, with distinct quadrants corresponding to different anomaly types: conveyor anomalies were mainly distributed in the second quadrant, and chain belt anomalies clustered in the fourth. These results demonstrate that quantum kernel methods can achieve robust, multi class anomaly detection in noisy factory environments using minimal non contact sensors. This represents a significant advancement toward quantum enhanced smart factories with reduced sensing infrastructure and improved maintenance efficiency. This work has been accepted for presentation at IEEE QCE25 and will appear in the IEEE Xplore Digital Library."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"9 pages, 9 figures, 2 tables"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05594","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2025-10-08T01:52:30Z","registered":"2025-10-08T01:52:31Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-13T03:21:16Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05176","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05176","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05176","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ji","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Ji","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yiwei","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Yiwei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shaoxiong","familyName":"Feng","name":"Feng, Shaoxiong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Peiwen","familyName":"Yuan","name":"Yuan, Peiwen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xinglin","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Xinglin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiayi","familyName":"Shi","name":"Shi, Jiayi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yueqi","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Yueqi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chuyi","familyName":"Tan","name":"Tan, Chuyi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Boyuan","familyName":"Pan","name":"Pan, Boyuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yao","familyName":"Hu","name":"Hu, Yao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kan","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Kan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"PatternKV: Flattening KV Representation Expands Quantization Headroom"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-05T12:09:14Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:01:42Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-01-31T06:41:56Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02-03T01:30:55Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"KV cache in autoregressive LLMs eliminates redundant recomputation but has emerged as the dominant memory and bandwidth bottleneck during inference, notably with long contexts and test-time scaling. KV quantization is a key lever for reducing cache cost, but accuracy drops sharply as the native KV distribution lacks flatness and thus maintains a wide quantization range. Prior work focuses on isolating outliers, which caps their error but fails to flatten the overall distribution, leaving performance fragile under low-bit settings. In this work, we show that the K cache maintains a stable, context-evolving structure, while the V cache carries latent semantic regularities, with both contributing to the organization of vectors into shared patterns. Building on these insights, we propose PatternKV, a pattern-aligned residual quantization scheme. It mines representative pattern vectors online, aligns each KV vector to its nearest pattern, and quantizes only the residual. This reshaping of the KV distribution flattens the quantization target and narrows its range, thereby improving the fidelity of low-bit KV quantization. Across long-context and test-time scaling settings on multiple backbones, PatternKV delivers consistent 2-bit gains, with a 0.08% average 4-bit drop relative to FP16, improves test-time scaling accuracy by 10% on average, and raises throughput by 1.5x while supporting 1.25x larger batches."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05176","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T01:42:50Z","registered":"2025-10-08T01:42:51Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-03T05:41:07Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06213","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06213","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06213","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Albert","familyName":"Catalan-Tatjer","name":"Catalan-Tatjer, Albert","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Niccolò","familyName":"Ajroldi","name":"Ajroldi, Niccolò","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jonas","familyName":"Geiping","name":"Geiping, Jonas","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Training Dynamics Impact Post-Training Quantization Robustness"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T17:59:07Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-01-30T01:07:11Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-01-30T09:50:22Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-02-02T01:40:37Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"While post-training quantization is widely adopted for efficient deployment of large language models, the mechanisms underlying quantization robustness remain unclear. We conduct a comprehensive analysis of quantization degradation across open-source language model training trajectories up to 32B parameters and 15T training tokens to accurately assess the relationship between training dynamics and quantization performance. Our key finding is that quantization errors in large-scale training runs are driven by a complex interplay between learning rate and other training hyperparameters. Specifically, once learning rates decay, validation loss and quantization error diverge, largely independent of training data scale. To investigate interventions on the training dynamics and identify specific configurations that can modulate quantization robustness favorably, we train our own models in controlled experiments up to 100B tokens. Our results challenge the assumption that increasing dataset scale inherently compromises quantization effectiveness, demonstrating instead that strategic training hyperparameter interventions can improve quantization quality at scale."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06213","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T02:07:02Z","registered":"2025-10-08T02:07:02Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-02-02T03:25:19Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05580","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05580","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05580","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chen","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Chen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhantao","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Zhantao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Han","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Han","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fangyi","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Fangyi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chenchen","familyName":"Zhu","name":"Zhu, Chenchen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Anudeepsekhar","familyName":"Bolimera","name":"Bolimera, Anudeepsekhar","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Marios","familyName":"Savvides","name":"Savvides, Marios","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"MetaVLA: Unified Meta Co-training For Efficient Embodied Adaption"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T04:54:39Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:24:53Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-01-27T08:17:08Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-01-28T01:37:10Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-01-28T03:51:32Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-01-29T01:19:44Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Vision-Language-Action (VLA) models show promise in embodied reasoning, yet remain far from true generalists-they often require task-specific fine-tuning, incur high compute costs, and generalize poorly to unseen tasks. We propose MetaVLA, a unified, backbone-agnostic post-training framework for efficient and scalable alignment. MetaVLA introduces Context-Aware Meta Co-Training, which consolidates diverse target tasks into a single fine-tuning stage while leveraging structurally diverse auxiliary tasks to improve in-domain generalization. Unlike naive multi-task SFT, MetaVLA integrates a lightweight meta-learning mechanism-derived from Attentive Neural Processes-to enable rapid adaptation from diverse contexts with minimal architectural change or inference overhead. On the LIBERO benchmark, MetaVLA with six auxiliary tasks outperforms OpenVLA by up to 8.0% on long-horizon tasks, reduces training steps from 240K to 75K, and cuts GPU time by ~76%. These results show that scalable, low-resource post-training is achievable-paving the way toward general-purpose embodied agents. Code will be available."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05580","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T01:52:11Z","registered":"2025-10-08T01:52:11Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-01-29T03:13:19Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05274","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05274","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05274","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"D.","familyName":"Simeoni","name":"Simeoni, D.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"G.","familyName":"Parise","name":"Parise, G.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"A. R.","familyName":"Rossi","name":"Rossi, A. R.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"A.","familyName":"Frazzitta","name":"Frazzitta, A.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"F.","familyName":"Guglietta","name":"Guglietta, F.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"M.","familyName":"Sbragaglia","name":"Sbragaglia, M.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"A note on thermal effects in non-linear models for plasma-based acceleration"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Plasma Physics (physics.plasm-ph)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Accelerator Physics (physics.acc-ph)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T18:42:15Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:05:34Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-01-26T12:53:51Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-01-27T02:27:07Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We investigate the impact of a non-negligible background temperature on relativistic plasma wake-fields generated when a beam of charged particles passes through a neutral plasma at rest. We focus on the blowout regime, wherein the plasma response is highly non-linear: plasma electrons are radially blown out and expelled away from the propagation axis of the beam particles, creating a region (bubble) of ions without electrons. Our study builds upon earlier investigations for non-linear models of plasma wakefields developed in the limit of zero background temperature (Lu et al., Phys. Rev. Lett. 96, 165002 (2006)). In the presence of a non-zero background temperature, we characterize the model and focus on its predictions for the bubble size and the electromagnetic fields inside the bubble. Model predictions are studied in combination with PIC simulations."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"12 pages, 8 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05274","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T01:45:08Z","registered":"2025-10-08T01:45:09Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-01-27T04:35:48Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05574","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05574","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05574","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Erick","familyName":"Lee-Guzmán","name":"Lee-Guzmán, Erick","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Egor A.","familyName":"Maximenko","name":"Maximenko, Egor A.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Enrique Abdeel","familyName":"Muñoz-de-la-Colina","name":"Muñoz-de-la-Colina, Enrique Abdeel","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Marco Iván","familyName":"Ruiz-Carmona","name":"Ruiz-Carmona, Marco Iván","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Locally similar distances and equality of the induced intrinsic distances"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Metric Geometry (math.MG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Functional Analysis (math.FA)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"51K05, 30F45, 46E22","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"MSC"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T04:33:50Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:24:02Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2026-01-18T22:54:31Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-01-21T02:00:36Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Let $X$ be a set and $d_1,d_2$ be two distances on $X$. We say that $d_1$ and $d_2$ are locally similar and write $d_1\\cong d_2$ if $d_1$ and $d_2$ are topologically equivalent and, for every $a$ in $X$, \\[ \\lim_{x\\to a} \\frac{d_2(x,a)}{d_1(x,a)}=1. \\] We prove that if $d_1\\cong d_2$, then the intrinsic distances induced by $d_1$ and $d_2$ coincide. We also provide sufficient conditions for $d_1\\cong d_2$ and consider several examples related to reproducing kernel Hilbert spaces."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"32 pages, 6 figures. In the second version, we have changed the order of sections and added more examples"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05574","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T01:52:02Z","registered":"2025-10-08T01:52:03Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-01-21T06:06:04Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05759","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05759","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05759","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zexin","familyName":"Zheng","name":"Zheng, Zexin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Huangyu","familyName":"Dai","name":"Dai, Huangyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lingtao","familyName":"Mao","name":"Mao, Lingtao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xinyu","familyName":"Sun","name":"Sun, Xinyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zihan","familyName":"Liang","name":"Liang, Zihan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ben","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Ben","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuqing","familyName":"Ding","name":"Ding, Yuqing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chenyi","familyName":"Lei","name":"Lei, Chenyi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wenwu","familyName":"Ou","name":"Ou, Wenwu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Han","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Han","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kun","familyName":"Gai","name":"Gai, Kun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"OneVision: An End-to-End Generative Framework for Multi-view E-commerce Vision Search"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T10:25:21Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:39:13Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T09:02:25Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-09T00:34:40Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-11-01T09:20:55Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-11-04T01:26:17Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-01-08T04:15:41Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2026-01-09T01:19:36Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v4"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"4","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Traditional vision search, similar to search and recommendation systems, follows the multi-stage cascading architecture (MCA) paradigm to balance efficiency and conversion. Specifically, the query image undergoes feature extraction, recall, pre-ranking, and ranking stages, ultimately presenting the user with semantically similar products that meet their preferences. This multi-view representation discrepancy of the same object in the query and the optimization objective collide across these stages, making it difficult to achieve Pareto optimality in both user experience and conversion. In this paper, an end-to-end generative framework, OneVision, is proposed to address these problems. OneVision builds on VRQ, a vision-aligned residual quantization encoding, which can align the vastly different representations of an object across multiple viewpoints while preserving the distinctive features of each product as much as possible. Then a multi-stage semantic alignment scheme is adopted to maintain strong visual similarity priors while effectively incorporating user-specific information for personalized preference generation. In offline evaluations, OneVision performs on par with online MCA, while improving inference efficiency by 21% through dynamic pruning. In A/B tests, it achieves significant online improvements: +2.15% item CTR, +2.27% CVR, and +3.12% order volume. These results demonstrate that a semantic ID centric, generative architecture can unify retrieval and personalization while simplifying the serving pathway."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05759","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":3,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T01:56:20Z","registered":"2025-10-08T01:56:21Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-01-09T03:10:33Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05496","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05496","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05496","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tadashi","familyName":"Wadayama","name":"Wadayama, Tadashi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Mutual Information Estimation via Score-to-Fisher Bridge for Nonlinear Gaussian Noise Channels"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Information Theory (cs.IT)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T01:27:28Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:17:32Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-18T04:26:52Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-21T00:20:44Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2026-01-05T04:17:17Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2026-01-06T01:58:13Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We present a numerical method to evaluate mutual information (MI) in nonlinear Gaussian noise channels by using denoising score matching (DSM) learning for estimating the score function of channel output. Via de Bruijn's identity, Fisher information estimated from the learned score function yields accurate estimates of MI through a Fisher integral representation for a variety of priors and channel nonlinearities. In this work, we propose a comprehensive theoretical foundation for the Score-to-Fisher bridge methodology, along with practical guidelines for its implementation. We also conduct extensive validation experiments, comparing our approach with closed-form solutions and a kernel density estimation baseline. The results of our numerical experiments demonstrate that the proposed method is both practical and efficient for MI estimation in nonlinear Gaussian noise channels. Additionally, we discuss the theoretical connections between our score-based framework and thermodynamic concepts, such as partition function estimation and optimal transport."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05496","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T01:50:13Z","registered":"2025-10-08T01:50:14Z","published":null,"updated":"2026-01-06T04:10:25Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04841","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04841","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04841","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jihong","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Jihong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tommy","familyName":"Ohlsson","name":"Ohlsson, Tommy","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sampsa","familyName":"Vihonen","name":"Vihonen, Sampsa","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shun","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Shun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"One-Loop Effects in the Neutrino Matter Potential and Implications for Non-Standard Interactions"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"High Energy Physics - Phenomenology (hep-ph)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T14:25:57Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:36:31Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-12-17T17:42:55Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-12-18T01:56:10Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1103/3r2b-1hcc","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"In this work, we emphasize that it is necessary to take into account one-loop corrections of $2.0\\%$ to the neutrino matter potential in the precision measurements of neutrino oscillation parameters and in the experimental searches for new physics beyond the Standard Model. With the numerical simulation of the DUNE experiment, we study how radiative corrections to the matter potential affect neutrino oscillation probabilities, and thus, the event rates in the presence of neutrino non-standard interactions (NSIs). We find that neglecting one-loop corrections may lead to wrong conclusions for the discovery of NSIs. The implications for the determination of neutrino mass ordering and constraints on the NSI parameters in future long-baseline accelerator neutrino experiments are explored in a quantitative way."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"17 pages, 7 figures, 1 table"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04841","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2025-10-07T16:55:36Z","registered":"2025-10-07T16:55:36Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-12-18T03:06:54Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05392","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05392","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05392","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Andrea","familyName":"Valassi","name":"Valassi, Andrea","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"New GPU developments in the Madgraph CUDACPP plugin: kernel splitting, helicity streams, cuBLAS color sums"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computational Physics (physics.comp-ph)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"High Energy Physics - Experiment (hep-ex)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"High Energy Physics - Phenomenology (hep-ph)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"C.1.2; D.1.3; G.3; I.6.8; J.2","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"ACM"},{"subject":"65C05, 81T18, 81V05","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"MSC"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T21:37:37Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:11:51Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-11-24T18:54:23Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-11-25T02:57:07Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-12-10T19:09:29Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-12-12T01:02:00Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The first production release of the CUDACPP plugin for the Madgraph5_aMC@NLO generator, which speeds up matrix element (ME) calculations for leading-order (LO) processes using a data parallel approach on vector CPUs and GPUs, was delivered in October 2024. This was described in previous publications by the team behind that effort. In this paper, I describe my work on some additional developments and optimizations of CUDACPP, mainly but not exclusively for GPUs. The new approach, which represents a major restructuring of the CUDACPP computational engine, primarily consists in splitting the ME calculation, previously performed using a single large GPU kernel, into many smaller kernels. A first batch of changes, involving the move to separate \"helicity streams\" and the optional offloading of QCD color sums to BLAS, was recently merged into a new CUDACPP release, in collaboration with my colleagues. Since then, I have completed a second batch of changes, involving the possibility to split the calculation into groups of Feynman diagrams in separate source code files. This new feature makes it possible to compute QCD matrix elements for physics processes with a larger number of final state gluons: in particular, I present the first performance results from CUDACPP for the $2\\!\\rightarrow\\!6$ process $gg\\!\\rightarrow\\!t\\bar{t}gggg$ on CPUs and GPUs and the $2\\!\\rightarrow\\!7$ process $gg\\!\\rightarrow\\!t\\bar{t}ggggg$ on CPUs, which involve over 15k and 230k Feynman diagrams, respectively. I also take this opportunity to describe in detail some previously undocumented features of the CUDACPP software, both in the GPU and vector CPU implementations."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"35 pages, 12 figures, 9 tables"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05392","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T01:47:51Z","registered":"2025-10-08T01:47:52Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-12-12T03:11:49Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05620","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05620","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05620","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Salah Eddine","familyName":"Choutri","name":"Choutri, Salah Eddine","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Prajwal","familyName":"Chauhan","name":"Chauhan, Prajwal","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Othmane","familyName":"Mazhar","name":"Mazhar, Othmane","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Saif Eddin","familyName":"Jabari","name":"Jabari, Saif Eddin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Monte Carlo-Type Neural Operator for Differential Equations"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Numerical Analysis (math.NA)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (stat.ML)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T07:07:04Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:28:37Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-12-02T22:52:17Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-12-04T01:10:55Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The Monte Carlo-type Neural Operator (MCNO) introduces a framework for learning solution operators of one-dimensional partial differential equations (PDEs) by directly learning the kernel function and approximating the associated integral operator using a Monte Carlo-type approach. Unlike Fourier Neural Operators (FNOs), which rely on spectral representations and assume translation-invariant kernels, MCNO makes no such assumptions. The kernel is represented as a learnable tensor over sampled input-output pairs, and sampling is performed once, uniformly at random from a discretized grid. This design enables generalization across multiple grid resolutions without relying on fixed global basis functions or repeated sampling during training, while an interpolation step maps between arbitrary input and output grids to further enhance flexibility. Experiments on standard 1D PDE benchmarks show that MCNO achieves competitive accuracy with efficient computational cost. We also provide a theoretical analysis proving that the Monte Carlo estimator yields a bounded bias and variance under mild regularity assumptions. This result holds in any spatial dimension, suggesting that MCNO may extend naturally beyond one-dimensional problems. More broadly, this work explores how Monte Carlo-type integration can be incorporated into neural operator frameworks for continuous-domain PDEs, providing a theoretically supported alternative to spectral methods (such as FNO) and to graph-based Monte Carlo approaches (such as the Graph Kernel Neural Operator, GNO)."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05620","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T01:53:06Z","registered":"2025-10-08T01:53:07Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-12-04T18:41:13Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05814","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05814","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05814","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yi-Hsin","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Yi-Hsin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mårten","familyName":"Sjöström","name":"Sjöström, Mårten","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sebastian","familyName":"Knorr","name":"Knorr, Sebastian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Thomas","familyName":"Sikora","name":"Sikora, Thomas","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Rasterized Steered Mixture of Experts for Efficient 2D Image Regression"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T11:32:44Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:41:43Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-11-18T13:46:56Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-11-19T01:50:08Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The Steered Mixture of Experts regression framework has demonstrated strong performance in image reconstruction, compression, denoising, and super-resolution. However, its high computational cost limits practical applications. This work introduces a rasterization-based optimization strategy that combines the efficiency of rasterized Gaussian kernel rendering with the edge-aware gating mechanism of the Steered Mixture of Experts. The proposed method is designed to accelerate two-dimensional image regression while maintaining the model's inherent sparsity and reconstruction quality. By replacing global iterative optimization with a rasterized formulation, the method achieves significantly faster parameter updates and more memory-efficient model representations. In addition, the proposed framework supports applications such as native super-resolution and image denoising, which are not directly achievable with standard rasterized Gaussian kernel approaches. The combination of fast rasterized optimization with the edge-aware structure of the Steered Mixture of Experts provides a new balance between computational efficiency and reconstruction fidelity for two-dimensional image processing tasks."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05814","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T01:57:37Z","registered":"2025-10-08T01:57:38Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-11-19T03:17:57Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06190","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06190","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06190","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chenxiao","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Chenxiao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Cai","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Cai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"David","familyName":"Wipf","name":"Wipf, David","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhiyuan","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Zhiyuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"On Powerful Ways to Generate: Autoregression, Diffusion, and Beyond"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T17:49:30Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T01:02:50Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-11-17T03:58:26Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-11-18T02:22:28Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Diffusion language models have recently emerged as a competitive alternative to autoregressive language models. Beyond next-token generation, they are more efficient and flexible by enabling parallel and any-order token generation. However, despite empirical successes, their computational power and fundamental limitations remain poorly understood. In this paper, we formally study whether non-autoregressive generation in Masked Diffusion Models (MDM) enables solving problems beyond the reach of Auto-Regressive Models (ARM). Our results show that MDM with sufficiently large context length is computationally universal with decoding steps matching the optimal parallel time complexity in PRAM. However, when controlling for other factors, MDM's flexibility to generate in any-order does not expand what ARM can already solve. To address this, we propose a new form of generation called any-process generation, which extends MDM with capabilities to remask, insert and delete tokens, allowing self-correction, length-variable editing, and adaptive parallelism. Theoretically and empirically, we demonstrate these capabilities enable scalability to significantly harder reasoning problems that are otherwise intractable for ARM and vanilla MDM. Additionally, they prove essential for generation tasks where objects naturally evolve through non-sequential processes, crucial for extending current LLMs beyond natural language to domains such as coding and science."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06190","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T02:06:29Z","registered":"2025-10-08T02:06:30Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-11-18T05:16:00Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06216","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06216","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06216","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mert","familyName":"Kiray","name":"Kiray, Mert","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alican","familyName":"Karaomer","name":"Karaomer, Alican","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Benjamin","familyName":"Busam","name":"Busam, Benjamin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Dropping the D: RGB-D SLAM Without the Depth Sensor"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T17:59:30Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T01:04:01Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-11-02T21:12:24Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-11-04T02:06:23Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We present DropD-SLAM, a real-time monocular SLAM system that achieves RGB-D-level accuracy without relying on depth sensors. The system replaces active depth input with three pretrained vision modules: a monocular metric depth estimator, a learned keypoint detector, and an instance segmentation network. Dynamic objects are suppressed using dilated instance masks, while static keypoints are assigned predicted depth values and backprojected into 3D to form metrically scaled features. These are processed by an unmodified RGB-D SLAM back end for tracking and mapping. On the TUM RGB-D benchmark, DropD-SLAM attains 7.4 cm mean ATE on static sequences and 1.8 cm on dynamic sequences, matching or surpassing state-of-the-art RGB-D methods while operating at 22 FPS on a single GPU. These results suggest that modern pretrained vision models can replace active depth sensors as reliable, real-time sources of metric scale, marking a step toward simpler and more cost-effective SLAM systems."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06216","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T02:07:06Z","registered":"2025-10-08T02:07:07Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-11-04T04:58:02Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05175","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05175","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05175","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dmitry","familyName":"Rybin","name":"Rybin, Dmitry","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yushun","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Yushun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ding","familyName":"Tian","name":"Tian, Ding","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhihang","familyName":"Lin","name":"Lin, Zhihang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhi-Quan","familyName":"Luo","name":"Luo, Zhi-Quan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Exact Causal Attention with 10% Fewer Operations"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Discrete Mathematics (cs.DM)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Data Structures and Algorithms (cs.DS)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-05T11:53:58Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:01:41Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T09:39:20Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-10T00:43:52Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-11T06:44:12Z","dateType":"Withdrawn","dateInformation":"v3; Withdrawn to further refine claims about experiments and applications"},{"date":"2025-10-14T00:25:37Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v3"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"3","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial No Derivatives 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-nd-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We present Exact Causal Attention (ECA), a Strassen-style algorithm that computes exact Causal Attention using 10\\% fewer operations. ECA improves a special class of matrix multiplications where either one operand or the output matrix is upper- or lower-triangular. This includes all matrix multiplication operations in the forward and backward pass of Causal Attention, such as masked product $\\mathrm{Mask}(QK^{T})$. ECA is built upon algebraic identities discovered via machine learning and combinatorial search. We note that ECA cannot accelerate fused kernels such as FlashAttention on GPU. This is because ECA requires materialization of large intermediate expressions in the memory, while FlashAttention does not. However, it provides an alternative approach for compute-bound applications and can potentially be useful in scenarios with FLOPs considerations."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Withdrawn to further refine claims about experiments and applications"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05175","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":2,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T01:42:48Z","registered":"2025-10-08T01:42:49Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-14T04:23:48Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05587","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05587","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05587","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yiran","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Yiran","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ruican","familyName":"Ma","name":"Ma, Ruican","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haiyun","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Haiyun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dahai","familyName":"Yan","name":"Yan, Dahai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Gaussian process analysis of type-B quasiperiodic oscillations in the black hole X-ray binary MAXI J1348-630"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"High Energy Astrophysical Phenomena (astro-ph.HE)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T05:19:40Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-13T00:39:42Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We analyzed Insight-HXMT data of the black hole X-ray binary MAXI J1348-630 during the type-B QPO phase of its 2019 outburst. Using the Gaussian process method, we applied an additive composite kernel model consisting of an SHO, a DRW, and an additional white noise (AWN) to data from three energy bands: LE (1-10 keV), ME (10-30 keV), and HE (30-150 keV). We find that for the DRW component, correlations on the timescale of $τ_{\\rm DRW}\\sim10$ s are absent in the LE band, while they persist in the ME and HE bands over the full duration of the light curves. This energy-dependent behavior may reflect thermal instabilities, with the shorter correlation timescale in the disk compared to the corona. Alternatively, it may reflect variable Comptonizations of seed photons from different disk regions. Inner-disk photons are scattered by a small inner corona, producing soft X-rays. Outer-disk photons interact with an extended, jet-like corona, resulting in harder emission. The QPO is captured by an SHO component with a stable period of $\\sim 0.2$ s and a high quality factor of $\\sim 10$. The absence of significant evolution with energy or time of the SHO component suggests a connection between the accretion disk and the corona, which may be built by coherent oscillations of disk-corona driven by magnetorotational instability. The AWN components are present in all the three-band data and dominate over the DRW and SHO components. We interpret the AWN as another fast DRW with its $τ_{\\rm DRW} \u0026lt; 0.01$ s. It may trace high-frequency fluctuations that occur in both the inner region of the accretion disk and the corona. Overall, our work reveals a timescale hierarchy in the coupled disk-corona scenario: fast DRW \u0026lt; SHO \u0026lt; disk DRW \u0026lt; corona DRW."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted for publication in A\u0026amp;A, 10 pages, 8 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05587","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T01:52:20Z","registered":"2025-10-08T01:52:21Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-13T02:17:47Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05112","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05112","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05112","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lijuan","familyName":"Jiang","name":"Jiang, Lijuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xingjian","familyName":"Qian","name":"Qian, Xingjian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhenxiang","familyName":"Ma","name":"Ma, Zhenxiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zan","familyName":"Zong","name":"Zong, Zan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hengjie","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Hengjie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chao","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Chao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jidong","familyName":"Zhai","name":"Zhai, Jidong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"A Flexible Programmable Pipeline Parallelism Framework for Efficient DNN Training"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Distributed, Parallel, and Cluster Computing (cs.DC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-09-27T13:44:27Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:00:18Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-09T06:07:52Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10-10T00:32:05Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v2"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"2","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Pipeline parallelism is an essential distributed parallelism method. Increasingly complex and diverse DNN models necessitate meticulously customized pipeline schedules for performance. However, existing practices typically rely on predefined schedules, each with strengths, but fail to adapt automatically to the emerging model architectures. Exploring novel high-efficiency schedules is daunting due to the enormous and varying schedule space. Besides, manually implementing schedules can be challenging due to the onerous coding burdens and constantly changing needs. Unfortunately, existing frameworks have limitations in automated schedule exploration and lack flexibility and controllability.\n This paper presents FlexPipe, a programmable pipeline parallelism framework with enhanced productivity, programmability, debuggability, and ease of tuning. FlexPipe has two main components: a succinct domain-specific language (DSL) and an automated scheduler. FlexPipe enables automated schedule exploration for various parallel scenarios within a broad spectrum of schedule types at a small search cost. Besides, users can swiftly develop and customize schedules using the FlexPipe DSL, which embodies flexible controllability in the pipeline order of micro-batch computations over stages. It also provides convenient mechanisms to include new operations in schedules to meet changing demands. Our evaluation results demonstrate that FlexPipe achieves up to 2.28X performance speedup compared to the popular large-scale parallel framework Megtron-LM, and gains up to 1.49X performance speedup compared to the state-of-the-art automated pipeline parallelism framework."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05112","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":1,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T01:41:22Z","registered":"2025-10-08T01:41:23Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-10T02:39:35Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06181","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06181","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06181","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jinwen","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Jinwen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qin","familyName":"Lu","name":"Lu, Qin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Georgios B.","familyName":"Giannakis","name":"Giannakis, Georgios B.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Conformalized Gaussian processes for online uncertainty quantification over graphs"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Signal Processing (eess.SP)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (stat.ML)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T17:44:13Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T01:02:34Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial No Derivatives 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-nd-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Uncertainty quantification (UQ) over graphs arises in a number of safety-critical applications in network science. The Gaussian process (GP), as a classical Bayesian framework for UQ, has been developed to handle graph-structured data by devising topology-aware kernel functions. However, such GP-based approaches are limited not only by the prohibitive computational complexity, but also the strict modeling assumptions that might yield poor coverage, especially with labels arriving on the fly. To effect scalability, we devise a novel graph-aware parametric GP model by leveraging the random feature (RF)-based kernel approximation, which is amenable to efficient recursive Bayesian model updates. To further allow for adaptivity, an ensemble of graph-aware RF-based scalable GPs have been leveraged, with per-GP weight adapted to data arriving incrementally. To ensure valid coverage with robustness to model mis-specification, we wed the GP-based set predictors with the online conformal prediction framework, which post-processes the prediction sets using adaptive thresholds. Experimental results the proposed method yields improved coverage and efficient prediction sets over existing baselines by adaptively ensembling the GP models and setting the key threshold parameters in CP."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06181","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T02:06:16Z","registered":"2025-10-08T02:06:17Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-08T02:06:17Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06179","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06179","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06179","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Emre","familyName":"Adabag","name":"Adabag, Emre","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Marcus","familyName":"Greiff","name":"Greiff, Marcus","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"John","familyName":"Subosits","name":"Subosits, John","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Thomas","familyName":"Lew","name":"Lew, Thomas","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Differentiable Model Predictive Control on the GPU"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Optimization and Control (math.OC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Systems and Control (eess.SY)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T17:42:17Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T01:02:30Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial No Derivatives 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-nd-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Differentiable model predictive control (MPC) offers a powerful framework for combining learning and control. However, its adoption has been limited by the inherently sequential nature of traditional optimization algorithms, which are challenging to parallelize on modern computing hardware like GPUs. In this work, we tackle this bottleneck by introducing a GPU-accelerated differentiable optimization tool for MPC. This solver leverages sequential quadratic programming and a custom preconditioned conjugate gradient (PCG) routine with tridiagonal preconditioning to exploit the problem's structure and enable efficient parallelization. We demonstrate substantial speedups over CPU- and GPU-based baselines, significantly improving upon state-of-the-art training times on benchmark reinforcement learning and imitation learning tasks. Finally, we showcase the method on the challenging task of reinforcement learning for driving at the limits of handling, where it enables robust drifting of a Toyota Supra through water puddles."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06179","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T02:06:13Z","registered":"2025-10-08T02:06:14Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-08T02:06:14Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06175","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06175","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06175","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dingyu","familyName":"Yao","name":"Yao, Dingyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chenxu","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Chenxu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhengyang","familyName":"Tong","name":"Tong, Zhengyang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zheng","familyName":"Lin","name":"Lin, Zheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wei","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Wei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jian","familyName":"Luan","name":"Luan, Jian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Weiping","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Weiping","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"VecInfer: Efficient LLM Inference with Low-Bit KV Cache via Outlier-Suppressed Vector Quantization"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T17:35:28Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T01:01:59Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The Key-Value (KV) cache introduces substantial memory overhead during large language model (LLM) inference. Although existing vector quantization (VQ) methods reduce KV cache usage and provide flexible representational capacity across bit-widths, they suffer severe performance degradation at ultra-low bit-widths due to key cache outliers that hinder effective codebook utilization. To address this challenge, we propose VecInfer, a novel VQ method for aggressive KV cache compression while enabling efficient inference. By applying smooth and Hadamard transformations, VecInfer suppresses outliers in the key cache, enabling the codebook to comprehensively cover the original data distribution and thereby reducing quantization difficulty. To facilitate efficient deployment, we design an optimized CUDA kernel that fuses computation with dequantization to minimize memory access overhead. Extensive evaluations demonstrate that VecInfer consistently outperforms existing quantization baselines across both long-context understanding and mathematical reasoning tasks. With only 2-bit quantization, VecInfer achieves performance comparable to full precision, while delivering up to $\\mathbf{2.7\\times}$ speedup in large-batch self-attention computation and $\\mathbf{8.3\\times}$ reduction in single-batch end-to-end latency on Llama-3.1-8B with a 196k sequence length."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06175","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T02:06:07Z","registered":"2025-10-08T02:06:08Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-08T02:06:08Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06126","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06126","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06126","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haoxin","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Haoxin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaolong","familyName":"Tu","name":"Tu, Xiaolong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hongyu","familyName":"Ke","name":"Ke, Hongyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Huirong","familyName":"Chai","name":"Chai, Huirong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dawei","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Dawei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kyungtae","familyName":"Han","name":"Han, Kyungtae","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"lm-Meter: Unveiling Runtime Inference Latency for On-Device Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Performance (cs.PF)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T17:05:30Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:59:52Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1145/3769012.3770614","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large Language Models (LLMs) are increasingly integrated into everyday applications, but their prevalent cloud-based deployment raises growing concerns around data privacy and long-term sustainability. Running LLMs locally on mobile and edge devices (on-device LLMs) offers the promise of enhanced privacy, reliability, and reduced communication costs. However, realizing this vision remains challenging due to substantial memory and compute demands, as well as limited visibility into performance-efficiency trade-offs on resource-constrained hardware. We propose lm-Meter, the first lightweight, online latency profiler tailored for on-device LLM inference. lm-Meter captures fine-grained, real-time latency at both phase (e.g., embedding, prefill, decode, softmax, sampling) and kernel levels without auxiliary devices. We implement lm-Meter on commercial mobile platforms and demonstrate its high profiling accuracy with minimal system overhead, e.g., only 2.58% throughput reduction in prefill and 0.99% in decode under the most constrained Powersave governor. Leveraging lm-Meter, we conduct comprehensive empirical studies revealing phase- and kernel-level bottlenecks in on-device LLM inference, quantifying accuracy-efficiency trade-offs, and identifying systematic optimization opportunities. lm-Meter provides unprecedented visibility into the runtime behavior of LLMs on constrained platforms, laying the foundation for informed optimization and accelerating the democratization of on-device LLM systems. Code and tutorials are available at https://github.com/amai-gsu/LM-Meter."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"This is the preprint version of the paper accepted to The 10th ACM/IEEE Symposium on Edge Computing (SEC 2025)"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06126","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2025-10-08T02:04:58Z","registered":"2025-10-08T02:04:58Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-08T02:04:58Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06125","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06125","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06125","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Moumita","familyName":"Kamal","name":"Kamal, Moumita","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Douglas A.","familyName":"Talbert","name":"Talbert, Douglas A.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Downsized and Compromised?: Assessing the Faithfulness of Model Compression"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"I.2.6","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"ACM"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T17:05:02Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:59:51Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial No Derivatives 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-nd-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"In real-world applications, computational constraints often require transforming large models into smaller, more efficient versions through model compression. While these techniques aim to reduce size and computational cost without sacrificing performance, their evaluations have traditionally focused on the trade-off between size and accuracy, overlooking the aspect of model faithfulness. This limited view is insufficient for high-stakes domains like healthcare, finance, and criminal justice, where compressed models must remain faithful to the behavior of their original counterparts. This paper presents a novel approach to evaluating faithfulness in compressed models, moving beyond standard metrics. We introduce and demonstrate a set of faithfulness metrics that capture how model behavior changes post-compression. Our contributions include introducing techniques to assess predictive consistency between the original and compressed models using model agreement, and applying chi-squared tests to detect statistically significant changes in predictive patterns across both the overall dataset and demographic subgroups, thereby exposing shifts that aggregate fairness metrics may obscure. We demonstrate our approaches by applying quantization and pruning to artificial neural networks (ANNs) trained on three diverse and socially meaningful datasets. Our findings show that high accuracy does not guarantee faithfulness, and our statistical tests detect subtle yet significant shifts that are missed by standard metrics, such as Accuracy and Equalized Odds. The proposed metrics provide a practical and more direct method for ensuring that efficiency gains through compression do not compromise the fairness or faithfulness essential for trustworthy AI."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Submitted to and under review at Springer Machine Learning Journal"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06125","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T02:04:56Z","registered":"2025-10-08T02:04:57Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-08T02:04:57Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06051","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.06051","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.06051","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Farhad","familyName":"de Sousa","name":"de Sousa, Farhad","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"François","familyName":"Ribalet","name":"Ribalet, François","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jacob","familyName":"Bien","name":"Bien, Jacob","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Automated Gating for Flow Cytometry Data Using a Kernel-Smoothed EM Algorithm"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Methodology (stat.ME)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Applications (stat.AP)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation (stat.CO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T15:46:09Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:55:58Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Phytoplankton are microscopic algae responsible for roughly half of the world's photosynthesis that play a critical role in global carbon cycles and oxygen production, and measuring the abundance of their subtypes across a wide range of spatiotemporal scales is of great relevance to oceanography. High-frequency flow cytometry is a powerful technique in which oceanographers at sea can rapidly record the optical properties of tens of thousands of individual phytoplankton cells every few minutes. Identifying distinct subpopulations within these vast datasets (a process known as \"gating\") remains a major challenge and has largely been performed manually so far. In this paper, we introduce a fast, automated gating method, which accurately identifies phytoplankton populations by fitting a time-evolving mixture of Gaussians model using an expectation-maximization-like algorithm with kernel smoothing. We use simulated data to demonstrate the validity and robustness of this approach, and use oceanographic cruise data to highlight the method's ability to not only replicate but surpass expert manual gating. Finally, we provide the flowkernel R package, written in literate programming, that implements the algorithm efficiently."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.06051","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T02:03:14Z","registered":"2025-10-08T02:03:15Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-08T02:03:15Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05994","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05994","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05994","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhiliang","familyName":"Deng","name":"Deng, Zhiliang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhiyuan","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Zhiyuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaomei","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Xiaomei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaofei","familyName":"Guan","name":"Guan, Xiaofei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"A novel viewpoint for Bayesian inversion based on the Poisson point process"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Numerical Analysis (math.NA)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"35R30, 62F15, 86A22","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"MSC"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T14:53:41Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:52:56Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/zero/1.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Zero v1.0 Universal","rightsIdentifier":"cc0-1.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We present a novel Bayesian framework for inverse problems in which the pos terior distribution is interpreted as the intensity measure of a Poisson point process\n (PPP). The posterior density is approximated using kernel density estimation, and\n the superposition property of PPPs is then exploited to enable efficient sampling\n from each kernel component. This methodology offers a new means of exploring the\n posterior distribution and facilitates the generation of independent and identically\n distributed samples, thereby enhancing the analysis of inverse problem solutions."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"15 pages"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05994","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T02:01:54Z","registered":"2025-10-08T02:01:54Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-08T02:01:54Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05961","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05961","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05961","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Florian","familyName":"Buchner","name":"Buchner, Florian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Johannes","familyName":"Schörghuber","name":"Schörghuber, Johannes","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nico","familyName":"Unglert","name":"Unglert, Nico","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jesús","familyName":"Carrete","name":"Carrete, Jesús","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Georg K. H.","familyName":"Madsen","name":"Madsen, Georg K. H.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"msmJAX: Fast and Differentiable Electrostatics on the GPU in Python"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computational Physics (physics.comp-ph)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T14:18:00Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:51:02Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We present msmJAX, a Python package implementing the multilevel summation method with B-spline interpolation, a linear-scaling algorithm for efficiently evaluating electrostatic and other long-range interactions in particle-based simulations. Built on the JAX framework, msmJAX integrates naturally with the machine-learning methods that are transforming chemistry and materials science, while also serving as a powerful tool in its own right. It combines high performance with Python's accessibility, offers easy deployment on GPUs, and supports automatic differentiation. We outline the modular design of msmJAX, enabling users to adapt or extend the code, and present benchmarks and examples, including a verification of linear scaling, and demonstrations of its stability in molecular-dynamics simulations."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05961","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T02:01:07Z","registered":"2025-10-08T02:01:07Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-08T02:01:07Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05959","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05959","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05959","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kaixiang","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Kaixiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhaojian","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Zhaojian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wei","familyName":"Lin","name":"Lin, Wei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Distributed Platoon Control Under Quantization: Stability Analysis and Privacy Preservation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Systems and Control (eess.SY)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T14:16:59Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:50:57Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Distributed control of connected and automated vehicles has attracted considerable interest for its potential to improve traffic efficiency and safety. However, such control schemes require sharing privacy-sensitive vehicle data, which introduces risks of information leakage and potential malicious activities. This paper investigates the stability and privacy-preserving properties of distributed platoon control under two types of quantizers: deterministic and probabilistic. For deterministic quantization, we show that the resulting control strategy ensures the system errors remain uniformly ultimately bounded. Moreover, in the absence of auxiliary information, an eavesdropper cannot uniquely infer sensitive vehicle states. In contrast, the use of probabilistic quantization enables asymptotic convergence of the vehicle platoon in expectation with bounded variance. Importantly, probabilistic quantizers can satisfy differential privacy guarantees, thereby preserving privacy even when the eavesdropper possesses arbitrary auxiliary information. We further analyze the trade-off between control performance and privacy by formulating an optimization problem that characterizes the impact of the quantization step on both metrics. Numerical simulations are provided to illustrate the performance differences between the two quantization strategies."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"12 pages, 6 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05959","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T02:01:04Z","registered":"2025-10-08T02:01:05Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-08T02:01:05Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05943","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05943","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05943","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zheyue","familyName":"Tan","name":"Tan, Zheyue","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mustapha","familyName":"Abdullahi","name":"Abdullahi, Mustapha","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tuo","familyName":"Shi","name":"Shi, Tuo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Huining","familyName":"Yuan","name":"Yuan, Huining","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zelai","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Zelai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chao","familyName":"Yu","name":"Yu, Chao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Boxun","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Boxun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bo","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Bo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"EARL: Efficient Agentic Reinforcement Learning Systems for Large Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Distributed, Parallel, and Cluster Computing (cs.DC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T13:52:51Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:49:58Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Reinforcement learning (RL) has become a pivotal component of large language model (LLM) post-training, and agentic RL extends this paradigm to operate as agents through multi-turn interaction and tool use. Scaling such systems exposes two practical bottlenecks: (1) context length grows rapidly during training, inflating memory usage and latency, and triggering out-of-memory (OOM) failures; and (2) intermediate tensors accumulate with context length, making cross-device data movement a major system bottleneck.\n We present EARL, a scalable system for efficient agentic RL. EARL designs a parallelism selector that dynamically adapts model and training parallelism across RL stages based on sequence length and system load, and a data dispatcher that performs layout-aware, decentralized exchange of intermediate data batches. Together, these components increase throughput, reduce long-context failures, and enable stable large-scale training of agentic LLMs without relying on hard limits or penalties of context length."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05943","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T02:00:41Z","registered":"2025-10-08T02:00:42Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-08T02:00:42Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05918","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05918","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05918","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jakub","familyName":"Skowronski","name":"Skowronski, Jakub","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Axel","familyName":"Boeltzig","name":"Boeltzig, Axel","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Low-energy Cross Section Measurements of ${}^{\\mathsf{12}}\\mathsf{C}(\\mathsf{p},γ)$ Deep Underground at LUNA"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Instrumentation and Detectors (physics.ins-det)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Solar and Stellar Astrophysics (astro-ph.SR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Nuclear Experiment (nucl-ex)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T13:29:09Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:48:50Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1051/epjconf/202327903002","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The ${}^{\\mathsf{12}}\\mathsf{C}(\\mathsf{p},γ)$ reaction cross section is currently under investigation in the low-background environment of the Laboratory for Underground Nuclear Astrophysics (LUNA). It is being studied using different types of solid targets, and employing two complementary detection techniques: HPGe spectroscopy and activation counting. To reduce systematic uncertainties, targets have been accurately characterized and their degradation under the intense beam of the LUNA-400 accelerator monitored. We present the experimental techniques and the corresponding analyses used to extract the reaction cross section."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05918","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2025-10-08T02:00:05Z","registered":"2025-10-08T02:00:06Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-08T02:00:06Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05891","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05891","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05891","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yanran","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Yanran","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bingyao","familyName":"Yu","name":"Yu, Bingyao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yu","familyName":"Zheng","name":"Zheng, Yu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wenzhao","familyName":"Zheng","name":"Zheng, Wenzhao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yueqi","familyName":"Duan","name":"Duan, Yueqi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lei","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Lei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jie","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Jie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiwen","familyName":"Lu","name":"Lu, Jiwen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"$\\bf{D^3}$QE: Learning Discrete Distribution Discrepancy-aware Quantization Error for Autoregressive-Generated Image Detection"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T13:02:27Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:46:52Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The emergence of visual autoregressive (AR) models has revolutionized image generation while presenting new challenges for synthetic image detection. Unlike previous GAN or diffusion-based methods, AR models generate images through discrete token prediction, exhibiting both marked improvements in image synthesis quality and unique characteristics in their vector-quantized representations. In this paper, we propose to leverage Discrete Distribution Discrepancy-aware Quantization Error (D$^3$QE) for autoregressive-generated image detection that exploits the distinctive patterns and the frequency distribution bias of the codebook existing in real and fake images. We introduce a discrete distribution discrepancy-aware transformer that integrates dynamic codebook frequency statistics into its attention mechanism, fusing semantic features and quantization error latent. To evaluate our method, we construct a comprehensive dataset termed ARForensics covering 7 mainstream visual AR models. Experiments demonstrate superior detection accuracy and strong generalization of D$^3$QE across different AR models, with robustness to real-world perturbations. Code is available at \\href{https://github.com/Zhangyr2022/D3QE}{https://github.com/Zhangyr2022/D3QE}."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"10 pages, 5 figures, published to ICCV2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05891","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T01:59:25Z","registered":"2025-10-08T01:59:26Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-08T01:59:26Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05885","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05885","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05885","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alexis","familyName":"Montoison","name":"Montoison, Alexis","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"François","familyName":"Pacaud","name":"Pacaud, François","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Michael","familyName":"Saunders","name":"Saunders, Michael","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sungho","familyName":"Shin","name":"Shin, Sungho","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dominique","familyName":"Orban","name":"Orban, Dominique","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"MadNCL: A GPU Implementation of Algorithm NCL for Large-Scale, Degenerate Nonlinear Programs"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Optimization and Control (math.OC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T12:56:39Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:46:20Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We present a GPU implementation of Algorithm NCL, an augmented Lagrangian method for solving large-scale and degenerate nonlinear programs. Although interior-point methods and sequential quadratic programming are widely used for solving nonlinear programs, the augmented Lagrangian method is known to offer superior robustness against constraint degeneracies and can rapidly detect infeasibility. We introduce several enhancements to Algorithm NCL, including fusion of the inner and outer loops and use of extrapolation steps, which improve both efficiency and convergence stability. Further, NCL has the key advantage of being well-suited for GPU architectures because of the regularity of the KKT systems provided by quadratic penalty terms. In particular, the NCL subproblem formulation allows the KKT systems to be naturally expressed as either stabilized or condensed KKT systems, whereas the interior-point approach requires aggressive reformulations or relaxations to make it suitable for GPUs. Both systems can be efficiently solved on GPUs using sparse \\ldlt factorization with static pivoting, as implemented in NVIDIA cuDSS. Building on these advantages, we examine the KKT systems arising from NCL subproblems. We present an optimized GPU implementation of Algorithm NCL by leveraging MadNLP as an interior-point subproblem solver and utilizing the stabilized and condensed formulations of the KKT systems for computing Newton steps. Numerical experiments on various large-scale and degenerate NLPs, including optimal power flow, COPS benchmarks, and security-constrained optimal power flow, demonstrate that MadNCL operates efficiently on GPUs while effectively managing problem degeneracy, including MPCC constraints."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05885","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T01:59:16Z","registered":"2025-10-08T01:59:17Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-08T01:59:17Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05849","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05849","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05849","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Adhithyan","familyName":"Kalaivanan","name":"Kalaivanan, Adhithyan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zheng","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Zheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jens","familyName":"Sjölund","name":"Sjölund, Jens","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fredrik","familyName":"Lindsten","name":"Lindsten, Fredrik","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"ESS-Flow: Training-free guidance of flow-based models as inference in source space"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (stat.ML)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T12:11:58Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:43:35Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Guiding pretrained flow-based generative models for conditional generation or to produce samples with desired target properties enables solving diverse tasks without retraining on paired data. We present ESS-Flow, a gradient-free method that leverages the typically Gaussian prior of the source distribution in flow-based models to perform Bayesian inference directly in the source space using Elliptical Slice Sampling. ESS-Flow only requires forward passes through the generative model and observation process, no gradient or Jacobian computations, and is applicable even when gradients are unreliable or unavailable, such as with simulation-based observations or quantization in the generation or observation process. We demonstrate its effectiveness on designing materials with desired target properties and predicting protein structures from sparse inter-residue distance measurements."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"14 pages, 12 figures. Code will be made available after publication"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05849","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T01:58:27Z","registered":"2025-10-08T01:58:28Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-08T01:58:28Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05832","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05832","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05832","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kerem","familyName":"Semiz","name":"Semiz, Kerem","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"SPARTA: Python-Based Automated Spectral Parameter Analysis and Assessment System for Resonance Tracking"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Accelerator Physics (physics.acc-ph)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T11:56:13Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:42:40Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Accurately determining resonance frequencies and quality factors (Q) is crucial in accelerator physics and radiofrequency engineering, as these factors have direct impacts on system design, operational stability, and research results. The methods currently employed to facilitate resonance analysis are mostly manual, requiring operators and physicists to estimate resonant parameters and examine scattering parameter (S-parameter) data from vector network analyzers (VNAs). The current techniques therefore become laborious, operator-dependent and challenging to replicate when applied to large datasets or across multiple analyses. Despite the importance of these tasks for high-volume research organizations such as CERN where S-parameter measurements are regularly taken for cavity and beam diagnostic research, the currently widespread measures are outdated.\n In order to automate the laborious resonance characterization process, this paper presents SPARTA (Spectral Parameter Analysis for Resonance Tracking and Assessment), a data analysis software framework based on Python. SPARTA has integrated data ingestion, preprocessing, resonance detection, quality factor estimation, visualization and persistent cloud storage into a reproducible and scalable workflow. SPARTA was developed with the scientific libraries NumPy, SciPy and scikit-rf for accurate and efficient numerical processing, Flask and Dash for interactive and lightweight visualization, and SQLite for easy database management. The three main contributions of this work are outlined as follows: Firstly, this paper presents a methodological framework for resonance detection and assessment based on established RF theory. The second section describes the system architecture and implementation of SPARTA, highlighting data handling, computation and visualization. Finally this paper discusses the results and advantages of automated analysis."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05832","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T01:58:02Z","registered":"2025-10-08T01:58:03Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-08T01:58:03Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05801","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05801","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05801","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Josefa","familyName":"Caballero","name":"Caballero, Josefa","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Łukasz","familyName":"Płociniczak","name":"Płociniczak, Łukasz","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kishin","familyName":"Sadarangani","name":"Sadarangani, Kishin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Generalized capillary-rise models: existence and fast solvers in integral Hölder spaces"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Numerical Analysis (math.NA)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Classical Analysis and ODEs (math.CA)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T11:18:37Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:41:19Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We study a class of nonlinear Volterra integral equations that generalize the classical capillary rise models, allowing for nonsmooth kernels and nonlinearities. To accommodate such generalities, we work in two families of function spaces: spaces with prescribed modulus of continuity and integral Hölder spaces. We establish existence results for solutions within the integral Hölder space framework. Furthermore, we analyze the behavior of linear interpolation in these spaces and provide, for the first time, sharp error estimates, demonstrating their optimality. Building on this foundation, we propose a piecewise linear collocation method tailored to solutions in integral Hölder spaces and prove its convergence. For problems admitting smoother solutions, we develop an efficient spectral collocation scheme based on Legendre nodes. Finally, several numerical experiments illustrate the theoretical results and highlight the performance of the proposed methods."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05801","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T01:57:19Z","registered":"2025-10-08T01:57:20Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-08T01:57:20Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05651","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05651","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05651","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Eliana","familyName":"Masha","name":"Masha, Eliana","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Daniel","familyName":"Bemmerer","name":"Bemmerer, Daniel","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Axel","familyName":"Boeltzig","name":"Boeltzig, Axel","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Konrad","familyName":"Schmidt","name":"Schmidt, Konrad","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Anup","familyName":"Yadav","name":"Yadav, Anup","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Steffen","familyName":"Turkat","name":"Turkat, Steffen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kai","familyName":"Zuber","name":"Zuber, Kai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Underground nuclear astrophysics: Status and recent results from Felsenkeller laboratory"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Nuclear Experiment (nucl-ex)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T08:00:28Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:31:32Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1051/epjconf/202430101002","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"For almost three decades it has been known that the study of astro-physically important nuclear reactions between stable nuclei requires the use of low-background, underground accelerator laboratories. The Felsenkeller shallow-underground laboratory in Dresden, shielded by a 45 m thick rock cover, hosts a 5 MV Pelletron ion accelerator with an external sputter ion source (mainly able to provide carbon and oxygen beams) and an internal radio-frequency ion source (providing proton and alpha beams). The reduced muon, neutron and gamma-ray background achieved both with natural and active shielding situate the laboratory well in line with deep underground accelerator labs worldwide and allows highly sensitive nuclear reaction experiments. Currently, measurements affecting the solar fusion and Big Bang nucleosynthesis are ongoing. In addition to in-house research by HZDR and TU Dresden, the lab is an open facility for scientific users worldwide, with beam time applications reviewed by an independent science advisory board. Furthermore, EU-supported transnational access is available via the ChETEC- INFRA network for nuclear astrophysics. A brief introduction to underground nuclear astrophysics, status of the Felsenkeller shallow-underground laboratory and some preliminary results are discussed."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05651","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2025-10-08T01:53:50Z","registered":"2025-10-08T01:53:50Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-08T01:53:50Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05568","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05568","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05568","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nicholas H.","familyName":"Nelsen","name":"Nelsen, Nicholas H.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Houman","familyName":"Owhadi","name":"Owhadi, Houman","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Andrew M.","familyName":"Stuart","name":"Stuart, Andrew M.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xianjin","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Xianjin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zongren","familyName":"Zou","name":"Zou, Zongren","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Bilevel optimization for learning hyperparameters: Application to solving PDEs and inverse problems with Gaussian processes"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (stat.ML)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computational Physics (physics.comp-ph)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T04:22:09Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:23:49Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial No Derivatives 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-nd-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Methods for solving scientific computing and inference problems, such as kernel- and neural network-based approaches for partial differential equations (PDEs), inverse problems, and supervised learning tasks, depend crucially on the choice of hyperparameters. Specifically, the efficacy of such methods, and in particular their accuracy, stability, and generalization properties, strongly depends on the choice of hyperparameters. While bilevel optimization offers a principled framework for hyperparameter tuning, its nested optimization structure can be computationally demanding, especially in PDE-constrained contexts. In this paper, we propose an efficient strategy for hyperparameter optimization within the bilevel framework by employing a Gauss-Newton linearization of the inner optimization step. Our approach provides closed-form updates, eliminating the need for repeated costly PDE solves. As a result, each iteration of the outer loop reduces to a single linearized PDE solve, followed by explicit gradient-based hyperparameter updates. We demonstrate the effectiveness of the proposed method through Gaussian process models applied to nonlinear PDEs and to PDE inverse problems. Extensive numerical experiments highlight substantial improvements in accuracy and robustness compared to conventional random hyperparameter initialization. In particular, experiments with additive kernels and neural network-parameterized deep kernels demonstrate the method's scalability and effectiveness for high-dimensional hyperparameter optimization."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05568","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T01:51:54Z","registered":"2025-10-08T01:51:54Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-08T01:51:54Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05529","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05529","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05529","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Harshil","familyName":"Vejendla","name":"Vejendla, Harshil","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"H1B-KV: Hybrid One-Bit Caches for Memory-Efficient Large Language Model Inference"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T02:39:35Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:20:15Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Autoregressive decoding in large language models (LLMs) requires caching a growing list of past key-value (KV) pairs, making long-context inference a memory-bound problem. While recent methods have explored quantizing the cache, evicting tokens, or using binary sketches for keys (e.g., Loki), these approaches often provide an incomplete solution by leaving one component (like values) uncompressed or by discarding context information. This paper introduces the Hybrid One-Bit KV Cache (H1B-KV), a comprehensive compression scheme that radically reduces memory usage without sacrificing context. H1B-KV represents each key vector using a 1-bit binary sketch, enabling hardware-friendly bitwise attention, and further compresses value vectors using 4-bit quantization. This holistic, hybrid approach allows a 7-billion parameter LLM to handle an 8k-token context with under 60 MB of cache memory - a 70x reduction. We demonstrate that after a lightweight finetuning, H1B-KV matches full-precision performance not only on perplexity benchmarks but also on complex downstream tasks like mathematical reasoning (GSM8K), multi-task understanding (MMLU), and code generation (HumanEval). Our results show H1B-KV significantly outperforms leading quantization (KIVI), token eviction (SparseLLM), and key-only sketching (Loki) methods in quality-per-byte, establishing it as a robust solution for deploying LLMs in memory-constrained environments."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"MIT URTC 2025 Technical Paper (Oral), 5 pages, 1 figure"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05529","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T01:50:59Z","registered":"2025-10-08T01:51:00Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-08T01:51:00Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05511","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05511","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05511","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Aavid","familyName":"Mathrawala","name":"Mathrawala, Aavid","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dhruv","familyName":"Kurup","name":"Kurup, Dhruv","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Josie","familyName":"Lau","name":"Lau, Josie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"EEG-Based Acute Pain Classification: Machine Learning Model Comparison and Real-Time Clinical Feasibility"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T01:57:36Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:18:36Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Current pain assessment within hospitals often relies on self-reporting or non-specific EKG vital signs. This system leaves critically ill, sedated, and cognitively impaired patients vulnerable to undertreated pain and opioid overuse. Electroencephalography (EEG) offers a noninvasive method of measuring brain activity. This technology could potentially be applied as an assistive tool to highlight nociceptive processing in order to mitigate this issue. In this study, we compared machine learning models for classifying high-pain versus low/no-pain EEG epochs using data from fifty-two healthy adults exposed to laser-evoked pain at three intensities (low, medium, high). Each four-second epoch was transformed into a 537-feature vector spanning spectral power, band ratios, Hjorth parameters, entropy measures, coherence, wavelet energies, and peak-frequency metrics. Nine traditional machine learning models were evaluated with leave-one-participant-out cross-validation. A support vector machine with radial basis function kernel achieved the best offline performance with 88.9% accuracy and sub-millisecond inference time (1.02 ms). Our Feature importance analysis was consistent with current canonical pain physiology, showing contralateral alpha suppression, midline theta/alpha enhancement, and frontal gamma bursts. The real-time XGBoost model maintained an end-to-end latency of about 4 ms and 94.2% accuracy, demonstrating that an EEG-based pain monitor is technically feasible within a clinical setting and provides a pathway towards clinical validation."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05511","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T01:50:33Z","registered":"2025-10-08T01:50:34Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-08T01:50:34Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05485","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05485","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05485","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Adam","familyName":"Filipek","name":"Filipek, Adam","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"TensorBLEU: Vectorized GPU-based BLEU Score Implementation for Per-Sentence In-Training Evaluation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T01:02:46Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:17:00Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Modern natural language processing models have achieved unprecedented scale, yet the tools for their evaluation often remain a computational bottleneck, limiting the pace of research. This is particularly acute for in-training evaluation metrics, such as per-sentence reward signals in Reinforcement Learning, which must operate efficiently on batches of token IDs directly on the GPU. In this paper, we introduce TensorBLEU, a novel implementation of the BLEU metric designed from the ground up for this specific use case. Our approach is fully vectorized for GPU-accelerated, per-sentence computation within PyTorch and introduces a memory-efficient counting mechanism. By creating a compact, batch-specific dictionary of n-grams using \\texttt{torch.unique}, our method avoids the prohibitive memory costs of traditional hashing-based vectorization, making it practical for large-vocabulary models. We benchmark TensorBLEU against NLTK, the standard library for token-ID-based BLEU calculation on the CPU. Experiments show that TensorBLEU provides speedups of over 13x on consumer-grade GPUs (NVIDIA T4) and exceeding 40x on data-center-class hardware (NVIDIA A100). This performance transforms a significant bottleneck into a negligible part of the training loop. By clearly defining its role as a \"Token-ID BLEU\" for development purposes and open-sourcing our implementation, we provide a powerful tool for accelerating research in areas like RL-based model fine-tuning."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"9 pages, 3 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05485","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T01:49:58Z","registered":"2025-10-08T01:49:59Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-08T01:49:59Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05468","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05468","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05468","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yurun","familyName":"Song","name":"Song, Yurun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhuoyi","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Zhuoyi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ian G.","familyName":"Harris","name":"Harris, Ian G.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sangeetha Abdu","familyName":"Jyothi","name":"Jyothi, Sangeetha Abdu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"AMAQ: Adaptive Mixed-bit Activation Quantization for Collaborative Parameter Efficient Fine-tuning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-07T00:05:16Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:15:58Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large Language Models (LLMs) are scaling rapidly, creating significant challenges for collaborative server client distributed training, particularly in terms of communication efficiency and computational overheads. To address these challenges, we implement Parameter-efficient Split Learning, which effectively balances efficiency and performance for collaborative training on low-resource devices.\n To reduce communication overhead in collaborative training, we introduce Adaptive Mixed bit Activation Quantization (AMAQ), a strategy that progressively compresses activations and gradients from high precision (6 to 8 bits) to low precision (3 to 4 bits). AMAQ achieves this by effectively allocating bit budgets across channels based on feature wise and layer wise importance using bit regularization.\n Under the same bit budgets, AMAQ outperforms fixed-precision approaches, delivering about 2.5% higher generation accuracy and about 1.3% better classification accuracy for models like LLaMA3 8B and Qwen2.5 7B. In addition, it significantly enhances training stability and reducing ultra-low bit representation collapse during the training.\n Experiments demonstrate that AMAQ integrates effectively into practical multi-machine collaborative training setups, offering superior inference accuracy with only a modest communication overhead for bits adaptation during training. This trade off makes AMAQ a practical and effective solution for collaborative training with minimal communication cost."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"14 pages"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05468","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T01:49:35Z","registered":"2025-10-08T01:49:35Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-08T01:49:35Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05461","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05461","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05461","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nicolaus","familyName":"Kratochwil","name":"Kratochwil, Nicolaus","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Emilie","familyName":"Roncali","name":"Roncali, Emilie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Gerard","familyName":"Arino-Estrada","name":"Arino-Estrada, Gerard","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Analytic timing calculations and timing limits with prompt photons, high-aspect-ratio crystals, and complex TOF-kernels in TOF-PET"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Instrumentation and Detectors (physics.ins-det)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Medical Physics (physics.med-ph)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T23:47:30Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:15:36Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Modeling the timing performance of light-based radiation detectors accurately is essential for optimizing time-of-flight positron emission tomography (TOF-PET). We present an analytic framework that combines existing models to predict the timing behavior of high-aspect ratio crystals, including contributions from prompt photons such as Cherenkov radiation. This framework is built on a closed-form solution for optical light transport, convolved with the photodetector response and photon production characteristics. Using conditional and joint probability distributions, we compute the first-photon arrival time distribution for hybrid detectors with scintillation and Cherenkov light. The detection time distribution is then self-convolved to derive the time delay spectra and three timing metrics are used to characterize complex TOF kernels. Additionally, we perform Cramér-Rao Lower Bound calculations with and without depth-of-interaction bias to evaluate the theoretical timing limits. Our analytic predictions align well with Monte Carlo simulations for BGO detectors under varying crystal thicknesses and single photon time resolution considering a digital photodetector. We show that the TOF shape is significantly affected by prompt photon statistics, crystal thickness, scintillation yield, and photodetector properties resulting in distinct metric-dependent timing performance. The proposed model enables rapid timing predictions for polished crystals, with the calculation time of a detector configuration in under a second, allowing for comprehensive parametric studies. This makes it a powerful tool for guiding detector development in fast-timing applications."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"under review in IEEE TRPMS"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05461","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T01:49:25Z","registered":"2025-10-08T01:49:26Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-08T01:49:26Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05373","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05373","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05373","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Utkarsh","familyName":"Saxena","name":"Saxena, Utkarsh","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kaushik","familyName":"Roy","name":"Roy, Kaushik","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"KVLinC : KV Cache Quantization with Hadamard Rotation and Linear Correction"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T21:08:11Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:10:42Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Quantizing the key-value (KV) cache is a promising strategy for improving the inference efficiency of large language models (LLMs). However, aggressive quantization to very low precision (e.g., 2 bits) introduces significant errors in the stored key and value tensors, which propagate through the dot-product attention mechanism and ultimately degrade generation quality. To address this, we propose KVLinC, a framework to mitigate attention errors introduced by KV cache quantization in the extreme low-precision regime. KVLinC combines a Hadamard rotation, which reduces quantization error in values, with lightweight linear correction adapters that explicitly compensate for errors introduced by quantized keys. Across extensive evaluations on the LLaMA, Qwen2.5, and Qwen3 model families, KVLinC consistently matches or surpasses strong baselines while achieving higher KV-cache compression. Furthermore, we implement a custom attention kernel that results in upto 2.55x faster inference compared to Flash Attention baseline, enabling efficient long-context LLM inference."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"14 pages, 7 figures, 6 tables"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05373","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T01:47:25Z","registered":"2025-10-08T01:47:26Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-08T01:47:26Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05361","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05361","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05361","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alex","familyName":"Iacob","name":"Iacob, Alex","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Andrej","familyName":"Jovanovic","name":"Jovanovic, Andrej","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mher","familyName":"Safaryan","name":"Safaryan, Mher","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Meghdad","familyName":"Kurmanji","name":"Kurmanji, Meghdad","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lorenzo","familyName":"Sani","name":"Sani, Lorenzo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Samuel","familyName":"Horváth","name":"Horváth, Samuel","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"William F.","familyName":"Shen","name":"Shen, William F.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xinchi","familyName":"Qiu","name":"Qiu, Xinchi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nicholas D.","familyName":"Lane","name":"Lane, Nicholas D.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"MT-DAO: Multi-Timescale Distributed Adaptive Optimizers with Local Updates"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T20:37:57Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:10:04Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Training large models with distributed data parallelism (DDP) requires frequent communication of gradients across workers, which can saturate bandwidth. Infrequent communication strategies (e.g., Local SGD) reduce this overhead but, when applied to adaptive optimizers, often suffer a performance gap relative to fully synchronous DDP. We trace this gap to a time-scale mismatch: the optimizer's fast-moving momentum, tuned for frequent updates, decays too quickly to smooth gradients over long intervals, leading to noise-dominated optimization. To address this, we propose MT-DAO, a family of optimizers that employs multiple slow- and fast-moving first momenta or the gradient to track update dynamics across different time scales, for which we provide the first convergence guarantees. Empirically, for language-model pre-training, this eliminates the performance gap with DDP, outperforming infrequent-communication baselines in perplexity and reducing iso-token wall-clock time by 6-27% on Ethernet interconnects. At the 720M scale, MT-DAO reaches a target perplexity in 24% fewer steps and 35% less time than the single-momentum DDP baseline. MT-DAO enables effective cross-datacenter training and training over wide geographic areas."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Submitted to the ICLR 2026 Conference"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05361","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T01:47:09Z","registered":"2025-10-08T01:47:10Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-08T01:47:10Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05288","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05288","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05288","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ruoxing","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Ruoxing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"DP-Adam-AC: Privacy-preserving Fine-Tuning of Localizable Language Models Using Adam Optimization with Adaptive Clipping"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Cryptography and Security (cs.CR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T18:56:15Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:06:06Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large language models (LLMs) such as ChatGPT have evolved into powerful and ubiquitous tools. Fine-tuning on small datasets allows LLMs to acquire specialized skills for specific tasks efficiently. Although LLMs provide great utility in both general and task-specific use cases, they are limited by two security-related concerns. First, traditional LLM hardware requirements make them infeasible to run locally on consumer-grade devices. A remote network connection with the LLM provider's server is usually required, making the system vulnerable to network attacks. Second, fine-tuning an LLM for a sensitive task may involve sensitive data. Non-private fine-tuning algorithms produce models vulnerable to training data reproduction attacks. Our work addresses these security concerns by enhancing differentially private optimization algorithms and applying them to fine-tune localizable language models. We introduce adaptable gradient clipping along with other engineering enhancements to the standard DP-Adam optimizer to create DP-Adam-AC. We use our optimizer to fine-tune examples of two localizable LLM designs, small language model (Qwen2.5-0.5B) and 1.58 bit quantization (Bitnet-b1.58-2B). We demonstrate promising improvements in loss through experimentation with two synthetic datasets."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05288","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T01:45:28Z","registered":"2025-10-08T01:45:28Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-08T01:45:28Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05282","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05282","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05282","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xavier","familyName":"Andrade","name":"Andrade, Xavier","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jacopo","familyName":"Simoni","name":"Simoni, Jacopo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuan","familyName":"Ping","name":"Ping, Yuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tadashi","familyName":"Ogitsu","name":"Ogitsu, Tadashi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alfredo A.","familyName":"Correa","name":"Correa, Alfredo A.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"SHarmonic: A fast and accurate implementation of spherical harmonics for electronic-structure calculations"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computational Physics (physics.comp-ph)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Other Condensed Matter (cond-mat.other)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Mathematical Software (cs.MS)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Chemical Physics (physics.chem-ph)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T18:52:58Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:05:50Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The authors present SHarmonic, a new implementation of the spherical harmonics targeted for electronic-structure calculations. Their approach is to use explicit formulas for the harmonics written in terms of normalized Cartesian coordinates. This approach results in a code that is as precise as other implementations while being at least one order of magnitude more computationally efficient. The library can run on graphics processing units (GPUs) as well, achieving an additional order of magnitude in execution speed. This new implementation is simple to use and is provided under an open source license, it can be readily used by other codes to avoid the error-prone and cumbersome implementation of the spherical harmonics."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"13 pages, 2 figures. The code can be downloaded from https://gitlab.com/npneq/sharmonic"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05282","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T01:45:19Z","registered":"2025-10-08T01:45:20Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-08T01:45:20Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05277","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05277","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05277","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Daigo","familyName":"Ito","name":"Ito, Daigo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"John S.","familyName":"Nolan","name":"Nolan, John S.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Geometric Construction of Quiver Tensor Products"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Algebraic Geometry (math.AG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Category Theory (math.CT)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Representation Theory (math.RT)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"14F08, 16G20 (Primary), 14M25, 18M05 (Secondary)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"MSC"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T18:46:09Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:05:44Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial No Derivatives 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-nd-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"By a classic theorem of Beilinson, the perfect derived category $\\operatorname{Perf}(\\mathbb{P}^n)$ of projective space is equivalent to the category of derived representations of a certain quiver with relations. The vertex-wise tensor product of quiver representations corresponds to a symmetric monoidal structure $\\otimes_{\\mathsf{Q}}$ on $\\operatorname{Perf}(\\mathbb{P}^n)$. We prove that, for a certain choice of equivalence, the symmetric monoidal structure $\\otimes_{\\mathsf{Q}}$ may be described geometrically as an \\emph{extended convolution product} in the sense that the Fourier--Mukai kernel is given by the closure of the torus multiplication map in $(\\mathbb{P}^n)^3$. We also set up a general framework for such problems, allowing us to generalize the extended convolution description of quiver tensor products to the case where $\\mathbb{P}^n$ is replaced by any smooth complete toric variety of Bondal--Ruan type. Under toric mirror symmetry, this extended convolution product corresponds to the tensor product of constructible sheaves on a real torus. As another generalization of our results for $\\mathbb{P}^n$, we show that any finite-dimensional algebra $A$ gives rise to a monoidal structure $\\star_A'$ on $\\operatorname{Perf}(\\mathbb{P}(A))$, providing insights into the moduli of monoidal structures on $\\operatorname{Perf}(\\mathbb{P}^n)$."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"46 pages, comments welcome!"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05277","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T01:45:12Z","registered":"2025-10-08T01:45:13Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-08T01:45:13Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05260","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05260","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05260","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Organizational","affiliation":[],"name":"The CEPC Study Group","nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"CEPC Technical Design Report -- Reference Detector"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"High Energy Physics - Experiment (hep-ex)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T18:22:35Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:04:39Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The Circular Electron Positron Collider (CEPC) is a large international scientific project initiated by China's particle physicists to study the Higgs boson and perform critical tests of the Standard Model. Housed in a 100-km circumference tunnel in China, the CEPC will primarily operate as a Higgs factory, producing electron-positron collisions at a center-of-mass energy of 240 GeV. It will also function as a Z factory at 91.2 GeV and operate at the WW production threshold (around 160 GeV). In its baseline configuration, the CEPC produces two million Higgs bosons for one experiment. An upgraded scenario would enable higher luminosities, delivering 4.3 million Higgs events across two experiments. The CEPC will also generate trillions of Z bosons, whose subsequent decays will produce vast quantities of bottom quarks, charm quarks, and tau-leptons, establishing it as a high-precision B-factory and tau-charm factory. This document constitutes the second volume of the CEPC Technical Design Report (TDR). It provides a comprehensive description of the CEPC Reference Detector technical design. It summarizes the physics case for the CEPC, details the Reference Detector's technical design and technological options, and highlights its expected performance, demonstrating that the detector meets its design goals. A cost estimate for the Reference Detector and future development plans are also presented. Also included are two additional detector concepts, ILD and IDEA, developed by the international community for future electron-positron colliders and candidates to equip the CEPC's second interaction point. The first TDR volume, published in 2023, details the design of the CEPC accelerator complex. Pending government approval, construction is anticipated to begin around 2027-2028, with an estimated duration of eight years. Physics data-taking is projected to commence in the 2030s."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"705, to be published in Radiation Detection Technology and Methods"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05260","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T01:44:48Z","registered":"2025-10-08T01:44:48Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-08T01:44:48Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05254","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05254","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05254","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Filipp","familyName":"Sporykhin","name":"Sporykhin, Filipp","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Holger","familyName":"Homann","name":"Homann, Holger","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Performance of a high-order MPI-Kokkos accelerated fluid solver"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Distributed, Parallel, and Cluster Computing (cs.DC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T18:17:16Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:04:14Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1016/j.cpc.2025.109873","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"This work discusses the performance of a modern numerical scheme for fluid dynamical problems on modern high-performance computing architectures. Our code implements a spatial nodal discontinuous Galerkin scheme that we test up to an order of convergence of eight. It is temporally coupled to a set of Runge-Kutta methods of orders up to six. The code integrates the linear advection equations as well as the isothermal Euler equations in one, two, and three dimensions. In order to target modern hardware involving many-core Central Processing Units and accelerators such as Graphic Processing Units we use the Kokkos library in conjunction with the Message Passing Interface to run our single source code on various GPU systems. We find that the higher the order the faster is the code. Eighth-order simulations attain a given global error with much less computing time than third- or fourth-order simulations. The RK scheme has a smaller impact on the code performance and a classical fourth-order scheme seems to generally be a good choice. The code performs very well on all considered GPUs. The many-CPU performance is also very good and perfect weak scaling is observed up to many hundreds of CPU cores using MPI. We note that small grid-size simulations are faster on CPUs than on GPUs while GPUs win significantly over CPUs for simulations involving more than $10^7$ degrees of freedom ($\\approx 3100^2$ grid points). When it comes to the environmental impact of numerical simulations we estimate that GPUs consume less energy than CPUs for large grid-size simulations but more energy on small grids. We observe a tendency that the more modern is the GPU the larger needs to be the grid in order to use it efficiently. This yields a rebound effect because larger simulations need longer computing times and in turn more energy that is not compensated by the energy efficiency gain of the newer GPUs."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"12 pages, 16 figures. submitted to Computer Physics Communications"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05254","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2025-10-08T01:44:39Z","registered":"2025-10-08T01:44:40Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-08T01:44:40Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05245","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05245","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05245","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yue","familyName":"Pan","name":"Pan, Yue","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zihan","familyName":"Xia","name":"Xia, Zihan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Po-Kai","familyName":"Hsu","name":"Hsu, Po-Kai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lanxiang","familyName":"Hu","name":"Hu, Lanxiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hyungyo","familyName":"Kim","name":"Kim, Hyungyo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Janak","familyName":"Sharda","name":"Sharda, Janak","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Minxuan","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Minxuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nam Sung","familyName":"Kim","name":"Kim, Nam Sung","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shimeng","familyName":"Yu","name":"Yu, Shimeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tajana","familyName":"Rosing","name":"Rosing, Tajana","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mingu","familyName":"Kang","name":"Kang, Mingu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Stratum: System-Hardware Co-Design with Tiered Monolithic 3D-Stackable DRAM for Efficient MoE Serving"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Hardware Architecture (cs.AR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Emerging Technologies (cs.ET)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T18:09:47Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:03:46Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1145/3725843.3756043","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"As Large Language Models (LLMs) continue to evolve, Mixture of Experts (MoE) architecture has emerged as a prevailing design for achieving state-of-the-art performance across a wide range of tasks. MoE models use sparse gating to activate only a handful of expert sub-networks per input, achieving billion-parameter capacity with inference costs akin to much smaller models. However, such models often pose challenges for hardware deployment due to the massive data volume introduced by the MoE layers. To address the challenges of serving MoE models, we propose Stratum, a system-hardware co-design approach that combines the novel memory technology Monolithic 3D-Stackable DRAM (Mono3D DRAM), near-memory processing (NMP), and GPU acceleration. The logic and Mono3D DRAM dies are connected through hybrid bonding, whereas the Mono3D DRAM stack and GPU are interconnected via silicon interposer. Mono3D DRAM offers higher internal bandwidth than HBM thanks to the dense vertical interconnect pitch enabled by its monolithic structure, which supports implementations of higher-performance near-memory processing. Furthermore, we tackle the latency differences introduced by aggressive vertical scaling of Mono3D DRAM along the z-dimension by constructing internal memory tiers and assigning data across layers based on access likelihood, guided by topic-based expert usage prediction to boost NMP throughput. The Stratum system achieves up to 8.29x improvement in decoding throughput and 7.66x better energy efficiency across various benchmarks compared to GPU baselines."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05245","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2025-10-08T01:44:27Z","registered":"2025-10-08T01:44:28Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-08T01:44:28Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05186","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05186","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05186","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hongpei","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Hongpei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Han","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Han","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Huikang","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Huikang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dongdong","familyName":"Ge","name":"Ge, Dongdong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yinyu","familyName":"Ye","name":"Ye, Yinyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"OptPipe: Memory- and Scheduling-Optimized Pipeline Parallelism for LLM Training"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Distributed, Parallel, and Cluster Computing (cs.DC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Optimization and Control (math.OC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T01:06:33Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:01:55Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Pipeline parallelism (PP) has become a standard technique for scaling large language model (LLM) training across multiple devices. However, despite recent progress in reducing memory consumption through activation offloading, existing approaches remain largely heuristic and coarse-grained, often overlooking the fine-grained trade-offs between memory, computation, and scheduling latency. In this work, we revisit the pipeline scheduling problem from a principled optimization perspective. We observe that prevailing strategies either rely on static rules or aggressively offload activations without fully leveraging the interaction between memory constraints and scheduling efficiency. To address this, we formulate scheduling as a constrained optimization problem that jointly accounts for memory capacity, activation reuse, and pipeline bubble minimization. Solving this model yields fine-grained schedules that reduce pipeline bubbles while adhering to strict memory budgets. Our approach complements existing offloading techniques: whereas prior approaches trade memory for time in a fixed pattern, we dynamically optimize the tradeoff with respect to model structure and hardware configuration. Experimental results demonstrate that our method consistently improves both throughput and memory utilization. In particular, we reduce idle pipeline time by up to 50% under the same per-device memory limit, and in some cases, enable the training of larger models within limited memory budgets."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Use Mathematical Programming to model Pipeline Parallelism with Offloading to balance efficiency and memory requirement"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05186","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T01:43:04Z","registered":"2025-10-08T01:43:05Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-08T01:43:05Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05177","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05177","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05177","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jakub","familyName":"Frac","name":"Frac, Jakub","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alexander","familyName":"Schmatz","name":"Schmatz, Alexander","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qiang","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Qiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Guido","familyName":"Van Wingen","name":"Van Wingen, Guido","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shujian","familyName":"Yu","name":"Yu, Shujian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Adapting HFMCA to Graph Data: Self-Supervised Learning for Generalizable fMRI Representations"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Image and Video Processing (eess.IV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-05T12:35:01Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-08T00:01:43Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Functional magnetic resonance imaging (fMRI) analysis faces significant challenges due to limited dataset sizes and domain variability between studies. Traditional self-supervised learning methods inspired by computer vision often rely on positive and negative sample pairs, which can be problematic for neuroimaging data where defining appropriate contrasts is non-trivial. We propose adapting a recently developed Hierarchical Functional Maximal Correlation Algorithm (HFMCA) to graph-structured fMRI data, providing a theoretically grounded approach that measures statistical dependence via density ratio decomposition in a reproducing kernel Hilbert space (RKHS),and applies HFMCA-based pretraining to learn robust and generalizable representations. Evaluations across five neuroimaging datasets demonstrate that our adapted method produces competitive embeddings for various classification tasks and enables effective knowledge transfer to unseen datasets. Codebase and supplementary material can be found here: https://github.com/fr30/mri-eigenencoder"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05177","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-08T01:42:51Z","registered":"2025-10-08T01:42:52Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-08T01:42:52Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05100","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05100","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05100","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Stefan","familyName":"Roiser","name":"Roiser, Stefan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Robert","familyName":"Schöfbeck","name":"Schöfbeck, Robert","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zenny","familyName":"Wettersten","name":"Wettersten, Zenny","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Rapid event extraction and tensorial event adaption: Libraries for efficient access and generic reweighting of parton-level events and their implementation in the MadtRex module"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"High Energy Physics - Phenomenology (hep-ph)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"High Energy Physics - Experiment (hep-ex)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Accelerator Physics (physics.acc-ph)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T17:59:19Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:49:58Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We present Rex and teaRex, C++17 libraries for efficient management of parton-level hard scattering event information and completely generic reweighting of such events, respectively. Rex is primarily an interfacing and I/O library for Les Houches Event format files and provides an internal event format designed with data parallelism in mind, and teaRex extends this format to provide full parton-level reweighting functionality with minimal code needing to be written by the end user. These libraries serve as the foundation for the MadtRex reweighting module for MadGraph5_aMC@NLO, extending the functionality of the CUDACPP plugin to allow for data-parallel model-generic leading order parameter reweighting on SIMD-enabled CPUs and SIMT GPUs, speeding up reweighting by more than two orders of magnitude compared to MadGraph5_aMC@NLO running on the exact same hardware while providing trivial scalability to larger and distributed systems."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"34 pages, 5 figures, 6 algorithms"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05100","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T17:06:16Z","registered":"2025-10-07T17:06:17Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T17:06:17Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05047","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05047","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05047","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Víctor","familyName":"Blanco","name":"Blanco, Víctor","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Harshit","familyName":"Kothari","name":"Kothari, Harshit","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"James","familyName":"Luedtke","name":"Luedtke, James","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"A Unified Optimization Framework for Multiclass Classification with Structured Hyperplane Arrangements"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Optimization and Control (math.OC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T17:26:56Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:47:48Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"In this paper, we propose a new mathematical optimization model for multiclass classification based on arrangements of hyperplanes. Our approach preserves the core support vector machine (SVM) paradigm of maximizing class separation while minimizing misclassification errors, and it is computationally more efficient than a previous formulation. We present a kernel-based extension that allows it to construct nonlinear decision boundaries. Furthermore, we show how the framework can naturally incorporate alternative geometric structures, including classification trees, $\\ell_p$-SVMs, and models with discrete feature selection. To address large-scale instances, we develop a dynamic clustering matheuristic that leverages the proposed MIP formulation. Extensive computational experiments demonstrate the efficiency of the proposed model and dynamic clustering heuristic, and we report competitive classification performance on both synthetic datasets and real-world benchmarks from the UCI Machine Learning Repository, comparing our method with state-of-the-art implementations available in scikit-learn."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"28 pages, 2 tables, 9 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05047","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T17:04:06Z","registered":"2025-10-07T17:04:07Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T17:04:07Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05003","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.05003","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.05003","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Imran","familyName":"Mansha","name":"Mansha, Imran","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Resource-Efficient Fine-Tuning of LLaMA-3.2-3B for Medical Chain-of-Thought Reasoning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T16:42:11Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:45:31Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large Language Models (LLMs) such as GPT-4 and LLaMA have demonstrated remarkable reasoning abilities but require significant computational resources for fine-tuning. This paper presents a resource-efficient fine-tuning approach for LLaMA-3.2-3B to enhance medical chain-of-thought reasoning while operating under constrained GPU and memory settings. Using parameter-efficient tuning techniques such as LoRA and QLoRA, we adapt the base model on publicly available medical reasoning datasets. The model achieves improved reasoning coherence and factual accuracy while reducing memory usage by up to 60% compared to standard full fine-tuning. Experimental evaluation demonstrates that lightweight adaptations can retain strong reasoning capability in medical question-answering tasks. This work highlights practical strategies for deploying LLMs in low-resource research environments and provides insights into balancing efficiency and domain specialization for medical AI systems."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"6 pages, 2 figures. Submitted to arXiv for open access"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.05003","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T17:02:16Z","registered":"2025-10-07T17:02:17Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T17:02:17Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04999","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04999","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04999","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nilay","familyName":"Kumar","name":"Kumar, Nilay","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Priyansh","familyName":"Bhandari","name":"Bhandari, Priyansh","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"G.","familyName":"Maragatham","name":"Maragatham, G.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Bridging Text and Video Generation: A Survey"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Graphics (cs.GR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T16:39:05Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:45:15Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Text-to-video (T2V) generation technology holds potential to transform multiple domains such as education, marketing, entertainment, and assistive technologies for individuals with visual or reading comprehension challenges, by creating coherent visual content from natural language prompts. From its inception, the field has advanced from adversarial models to diffusion-based models, yielding higher-fidelity, temporally consistent outputs. Yet challenges persist, such as alignment, long-range coherence, and computational efficiency. Addressing this evolving landscape, we present a comprehensive survey of text-to-video generative models, tracing their development from early GANs and VAEs to hybrid Diffusion-Transformer (DiT) architectures, detailing how these models work, what limitations they addressed in their predecessors, and why shifts toward new architectural paradigms were necessary to overcome challenges in quality, coherence, and control. We provide a systematic account of the datasets, which the surveyed text-to-video models were trained and evaluated on, and, to support reproducibility and assess the accessibility of training such models, we detail their training configurations, including their hardware specifications, GPU counts, batch sizes, learning rates, optimizers, epochs, and other key hyperparameters. Further, we outline the evaluation metrics commonly used for evaluating such models and present their performance across standard benchmarks, while also discussing the limitations of these metrics and the emerging shift toward more holistic, perception-aligned evaluation strategies. Finally, drawing from our analysis, we outline the current open challenges and propose a few promising future directions, laying out a perspective for future researchers to explore and build upon in advancing T2V research and applications."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04999","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T17:02:06Z","registered":"2025-10-07T17:02:07Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T17:02:07Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04994","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04994","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04994","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sjoerd","familyName":"Dost","name":"Dost, Sjoerd","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"concurrentKanren: miniKanren for parallel execution"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Programming Languages (cs.PL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T16:30:30Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:45:01Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial No Derivatives 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-nd-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Concurrent logic programming predates miniKanren, but concurrent implementations of miniKanren have remained largely unexplored. In this work we present a parallel implementation of miniKanren in Go, demonstrating its feasibility and potential for performance improvements. Our approach leverages implicit parallelism allowing legacy programs to benefit from parallel execution. We discuss implementation strategies and evaluate the impact of parallelism, laying groundwork for future language-agnostic models."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"13 pages, 1 figure, for associated repo see https://github.com/deosjr/concurrentKanren"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04994","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T17:01:54Z","registered":"2025-10-07T17:01:55Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T17:01:55Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04976","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04976","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04976","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"A.","familyName":"Koujok","name":"Koujok, A.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"A.","familyName":"Hamadeh","name":"Hamadeh, A.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"L.","familyName":"Martins","name":"Martins, L.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"F.","familyName":"Kohl","name":"Kohl, F.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"B.","familyName":"Heinz","name":"Heinz, B.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"U.","familyName":"Ebels","name":"Ebels, U.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"P.","familyName":"Pirro","name":"Pirro, P.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Hybrid magnonic spintronic system for tunable broadband signal filtering and microwave generation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Applied Physics (physics.app-ph)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T16:15:19Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:43:57Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Non-conventional beyond-the-state-of-the-art signal processing schemes require parallelism, scalability, robustness and energy efficiency to meet the demands of complex data-driven applications. With further research, magnonic and spintronic circuits can potentially help to fulfill these requirements. We present an experimental proof-of-concept of a hybrid device that can employ broad deteriorated microwave signals to excite and detect low energy propagating spin waves (SWs). For this, we use the output signal of a spin-transfer torque nano-oscillator (STNO) and connect it to a RF filter based on a magnonic delay-line. The STNO serves as a tunable nano-scaled signal generator with a broad output linewidth. Its RF output is fed as input into the magnonic delay-line circuit. Tuning the magnetic field solely at the magnonic circuit, we demonstrate the capability to selectively filter a broad RF input, obtaining a spin-wave output signal with a much narrower linewidth. This allows to tune the frequency of the RF signal at the output simply by tuning the magnetic field. Our findings are a first step towards a versatile, energy-efficient and compact wave-based filter with high sensitivity. Such a device can use even low-power, degraded signals and convert them into tunable SW outputs, effectively reducing the need for charge-based signal processing."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04976","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T17:01:10Z","registered":"2025-10-07T17:01:10Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T17:01:10Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04890","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04890","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04890","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shihan","familyName":"Fang","name":"Fang, Shihan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wenxin","familyName":"Zheng","name":"Zheng, Wenxin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Retrofitting Control Flow Graphs in LLVM IR for Auto Vectorization"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Programming Languages (cs.PL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Hardware Architecture (cs.AR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Software Engineering (cs.SE)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T15:11:41Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:39:37Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Modern processors increasingly rely on SIMD instruction sets, such as AVX and RVV, to significantly enhance parallelism and computational performance. However, production-ready compilers like LLVM and GCC often fail to fully exploit available vectorization opportunities due to disjoint vectorization passes and limited extensibility. Although recent attempts in heuristics and intermediate representation (IR) designs have attempted to address these problems, efficiently simplifying control flow analysis and accurately identifying vectorization opportunities remain challenging tasks.\n To address these issues, we introduce a novel vectorization pipeline featuring two specialized IR extensions: SIR, which encodes high-level structural information, and VIR, which explicitly represents instruction dependencies through data dependency analysis. Leveraging the detailed dependency information provided by VIR, we develop a flexible and extensible vectorization framework. This approach substantially improves interoperability across vectorization passes and expands the search space for identifying isomorphic instructions, ultimately enhancing both the scope and efficiency of automatic vectorization. Experimental evaluations demonstrate that our proposed vectorization pipeline achieves significant performance improvements, delivering speedups of up to 53% and 58% compared to LLVM and GCC, respectively."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04890","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T16:57:34Z","registered":"2025-10-07T16:57:35Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T16:57:35Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04825","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04825","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04825","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Eleanor","familyName":"Jones","name":"Jones, Eleanor","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuji","familyName":"Nakatsukasa","name":"Nakatsukasa, Yuji","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"SubApSnap: Solving parameter-dependent linear systems with a snapshot and subsampling"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Numerical Analysis (math.NA)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Mathematics","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"65F99, 65F20","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"MSC"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T14:09:46Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:35:37Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"A growing number of problems in computational mathematics can be reduced to the solution of many linear systems that are related, often depending smoothly or slowly on a parameter $p$, that is, $A(p)x(p)=b(p)$. We introduce an efficient algorithm for solving such parameter-dependent linear systems for many values of $p$. The algorithm, which we call SubApSnap (for \\emph{Sub}sampled $A(p)$ times \\emph{Snap}shot), is based on combining ideas from model order reduction and randomised linear algebra: namely, taking a snapshot matrix, and solving the resulting tall-skinny least-squares problems using a subsampling-based dimension-reduction approach. We show that SubApSnap is a strict generalisation of the popular DEIM algorithm in nonlinear model order reduction. SubApSnap is a sublinear-time algorithm, and once the snapshot and subsampling are determined, it solves $A(p_*)x(p_*)=b(p_*)$ for a new value of $p_*$ at a dramatically improved speed: it does not even need to read the whole matrix $A(p_*)$ to solve the linear system for a new value of $p_*$. We prove under natural assumptions that, given a good subsampling and snapshot, SubApSnap yields solutions with small residual for all parameter values of interest. We illustrate the efficiency and performance of the algorithm with problems arising in PDEs, model reduction, and kernel ridge regression, where SubApSnap achieves speedups of many orders of magnitude over a standard solution; for example over $20,000\\times$ for a $10^7\\times 10^7$ problem, while providing good accuracy."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04825","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T16:54:56Z","registered":"2025-10-07T16:54:57Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T16:54:57Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04817","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04817","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04817","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Abhinav","familyName":"Madahar","name":"Madahar, Abhinav","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Natural Language Edge Labelling: Decoupling Intent from Execution in Structured LM Reasoning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T14:00:02Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:34:59Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Controllers for structured LM reasoning (e.g., Chain-of-Thought, self-consistency, and Tree-of-Thoughts) often entangle what to try next with how to execute it, exposing only coarse global knobs and yielding brittle, compute-inefficient, and hard-to-audit behavior. We introduce Natural Language Edge Labelling (NLEL), a labeller-tuner overlay that attaches a free-form natural-language directive to each search edge and translates it into a schema-bounded control vector for decoding, search (branch quotas, exploration $β$), generation bundle size, retrieval mixtures, and verification passes. A labeller $Λ$ emits labels from the parent state and a compact context; a tuner $Ψ$ maps $(P, L, C)\\to Π$, with strict schema validation and trust-region projection around safe defaults. Downstream selection remains ToT-style with score $S=μ+βσ$ and depth-annealed $β$. We show NLEL strictly generalizes CoT/ToT, prove an anytime-monotonicity property for top-$k$ selection under label-conditioned bundles, and bound selector shortfall by control-vector distortion, providing decision-relevant justification for guards like trust regions and verification passes. We instantiate $Ψ$ as a prompt-only JSON Parameter Emitter and preregister an evaluation on GSM8K, MATH (subset), StrategyQA, and ARC-Challenge with compute-aware reporting (success@compute, tokens-per-success) and ablations over $Λ$, $Ψ$, trust-region radius, and control quantization; preregistered forecasts anticipate accuracy gains at comparable token budgets and improved success@compute under constraints. NLEL offers an interpretable, model-agnostic interface that separates intent from execution for controllable, auditable LM inference."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04817","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T16:54:37Z","registered":"2025-10-07T16:54:38Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T16:54:38Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}}],"meta":{"total":73,"totalPages":1,"page":1},"links":{"self":"https://api.datacite.org/dois?client-id=arxiv.content\u0026query=registered%3A%5B2025-10-07T16%3A00%3A00Z+TO+2025-10-08T15%3A59%3A59.999Z%5D+AND+%28GPU+OR+GPUs+OR+kernel+OR+kernels+OR+accelerator+OR+accelerators+OR+%22distributed+training%22+OR+%22model+serving%22+OR+%22KV+cache%22+OR+%22KV-cache%22+OR+quantization+OR+parallelism+OR+compiler+OR+compilers%29\u0026page%5Bsize%5D=100\u0026page%5Bnumber%5D=1\u0026sort=registered"}}